kat.cr/user/Blink99/ Review of Pharmacology 136 Autacoids"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-e29fd197f6d4","stem":"Which of the following NSAID has good tissue pen- etrability with concentration in synovial fluid?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ketorolac"},{"label":"B","text":"Diclofenac sodium"},{"label":"C","text":"Sulindac"},{"label":"D","text":"Piroxicam"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-9deac87a5dd7","stem":"Most commonly used drug for prophylaxis of migraine is","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Sumatriptan"},{"label":"B","text":"Propranolol"},{"label":"C","text":"Valproate"},{"label":"D","text":"Flunarizine"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-be475362790e","stem":"All of the following drugs are reversible inhibitors of cyclo-oxygenase except","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Diclofenac"},{"label":"B","text":"Ibuprofen"},{"label":"C","text":"Aspirin"},{"label":"D","text":"Indomethacin"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-06173cf9297b","stem":"Which of the flowing drug causes irreversible inhibition of cyclooxygenase?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Naproxen"},{"label":"B","text":"Aspirin"},{"label":"C","text":"Indomethacin"},{"label":"D","text":"Acetaminophen"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-ea63ba8d0037","stem":"Which of the following drug is useful in prophylaxis of migraine?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Propranalol"},{"label":"B","text":"Sumatriptan"},{"label":"C","text":"Domperidone"},{"label":"D","text":"Ergotamine"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-85246ef75d18","stem":"Which of the following drug is useful in acute attack of migraine?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Bromocriptine"},{"label":"B","text":"Cinnarizine"},{"label":"C","text":"Sumatriptan"},{"label":"D","text":"Ondansetron"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-a18c1ac4d248","stem":"Which of the following H1 blocker has high anti-cholin- ergic activity","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cetrizine"},{"label":"B","text":"Chlorpheniramine"},{"label":"C","text":"Fexofenadine"},{"label":"D","text":"Astemizole"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-e66118579e06","stem":"A 15 year old boy was stung by a bee. He had difficulty in breathing and facial edema. What is the best treatment?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"IV Adrenaline"},{"label":"B","text":"SC Adrenaline"},{"label":"C","text":"IV Antihistamine"},{"label":"D","text":"IV Hydrocortisone"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-12c87b944c7e","stem":"All of the following are well known ototoxic drugs except","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Aspirin"},{"label":"B","text":"Acetaminophen"},{"label":"C","text":"Aminoglycosides"},{"label":"D","text":"Loop diuretics"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-69cb5bf30073","stem":"Which one is a contraindication to the use of ergot derivatives","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Migraine"},{"label":"B","text":"Hyperprolactinemia"},{"label":"C","text":"Obstructive vascular disease"},{"label":"D","text":"Postpartum hemorrhage"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-66ebda187c9f","stem":"Triptans used in migraine headaches are agonists at which receptor","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"5HTID/IB"},{"label":"B","text":"5HT1A"},{"label":"C","text":"5HT3"},{"label":"D","text":"5HT2A"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-76f2b9b35396","stem":"Which one of the following drug and active metabolite combination is incorrect","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hydroxyzine-Cetirizine"},{"label":"B","text":"Terfenadine-Fexofenadine"},{"label":"C","text":"Chlorpromazine-Promethazine"},{"label":"D","text":"Loratidine-Desloratidine"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-8e0ad41687c7","stem":"The drug which blocks both H1 and 5 HT2 receptor is","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Phenoxybenzamine"},{"label":"B","text":"Cyproheptadine"},{"label":"C","text":"Ritanserin"},{"label":"D","text":"Ondansetron"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-0867837ee348","stem":"Which of the following actions are performed by pros- tacyclin PGI2","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Vasodilation and platelet aggregation"},{"label":"B","text":"Vasodilation and inhibition of platelet aggregation"},{"label":"C","text":"Vasoconstriction and platelet aggregation"},{"label":"D","text":"Vasoconstriction and inhibition of platelet aggrega- tion"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"independent_no_source_key_bulk_v4","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"provisional_answer","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-a89e111422ca","stem":"All of the following are true about prostaglandin E2 ex- cept","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"It is the principal prostaglandin synthesized in the stomach"},{"label":"B","text":"Its biosynthesis is inhibited by aspirin"},{"label":"C","text":"It stimulates gastric acid secretion"},{"label":"D","text":"It causes dilatation of submucosa blood vessels"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-1c8cf8257b45","stem":"Latanoprost is used in treatment of","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Induction of labor"},{"label":"B","text":"Refraction"},{"label":"C","text":"Glaucoma"},{"label":"D","text":"Iridocyclitis https://kat.cr/user/Blink99/ Review of Pharmacology 138 Autacoids"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-f3ab5fff24a5","stem":"Ans (b) Cyclizine (Ref: Goodman and Gilman 12/e p921,922) Antihistaminics may be classified into first generation and second generation compounds on the basis of CNS penetration and anticholinergic properties. First Generation Anti-histaminics can penetrate blood brain barrier and possess additional anticholinergic properties which are lacking in second generation drugs. Cyclizine is a first generation whereas cetririzine, loratidine and fexofenadine are second generation drugs.","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Neurology","topicOriginal":"Neurology","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Neurology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-2cc4f738f019","stem":"Ans. (a) Promethazine (Ref: KDT 6/e p158) All the drugs listed in the question are second generation anti-histaminic agents except promethazine.","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-1871d77c9298","stem":"Ans. (c) 5-HT3 (Ref: KDT 6/e p163) All serotonin receptors are G-protein coupled receptors except 5-HT3, which is an inotropic receptor.","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-16887277b1a4","stem":"Ans. (d) Tegaserod (Ref: KDT 6/e p654) Both cisapride and tegaserod are selective 5HT4 agonists useful in the treatment of GERD. Cisapride possesses cardiac K+ channel blocking activity and can lead to torsades de pointes. Tegaserod is devoid of this adverse effect. However, tegas- erod has recently been withdrawn due to increased risk of MI and stroke.","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Cardiovascular","topicOriginal":"Cardiovascular","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Cardiovascular","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-4836fbb42857","stem":"Ans. (d) Treatment of these patients with erythromycin, a macrolide antibiotic (Ref: KDT 6/e p158) Astemizole and terfenadine are metabolized by microsomal enzymes. Erythromycin inhibits these enzymes and raises the plasma concentration of these drugs. At high levels, these agents block cardiac K+ channels leading to arrhythmias.","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Cardiovascular","topicOriginal":"Cardiovascular","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Cardiovascular","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-5268dc9c7397","stem":"Ans. (c) Diphenhydramine (Ref: Katzung 11/e p278) Diphenhydramine and promethazine have maximum anti-cholinergic activity and maximum ability to cross the blood brain barrier and are thus most effective in motion sickness.","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Neurology","topicOriginal":"Neurology","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Neurology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-ec1989694637","stem":"Ans. (d) Levetiracetam (Ref: Katzung 11/e p283) Prophylaxis of migraine is required if the attacks are frequent (>2-3 per month). The drugs useful for prophylaxis are: • Propanolol is the most commonly used drug for prophylaxis of migraine attacks. Timolol, atenolol, metopro- lol and nadolol can also be used but the drugs with ISA (e.g. pindolol) are ineffective. • Calcium channel blockers like flunarizine (also blocks Na+ channels) is also effective https://kat.cr/user/Blink99/ Review of Pharmacology 140 Autacoids • Methysergide, cyproheptadine and TCAs like amitriptyline can also be used. • Clonidine can be used orally for the prophylaxis of migraine attacks. • Topiramate (anticonvulsant drug) has recently been approved for prophylaxis of migraine. Valproate and gabapentin also possess some prophylactic activity.","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"CNS Pharmacology · Suggested","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":"CNS Pharmacology","options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"CNS Pharmacology · Suggested","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic","url_or_navigation_embedded"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-fb37bb2f4b93","stem":"Ans. (c) Nitroprusside (Ref: Goodman & Gilman 11/e p311) This agent is a mixed dilator and is highly effective agent against ergot induced vasospasm. It is also the drug of choice for most of the hypertensive emergencies.","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-bc60c446a7a4","stem":"Ans. (a) Prostaglandin E1 analogue (Ref: KDT 6/e p181) Natural prostaglandin in body is PGE2 whereas misoprostol and alprostadil are synthetic PGs which are PGE1 analogs.","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-b60f50e0b879","stem":"Ans. (d) Hepatotoxicity is likely to occur (Ref: KDT 6/e p198, 199) Ingestion of 10g (ten gram) of paracetamol will result in acute poisoning. Chances of death and fulminant hepatic failure are high if the plasma levels are above the line joining 200 μg/ml at 4 hours and 30μg/ml at 15 hours. Gastric lavage and activated charcoal at 6 hours will not be effective because most of the drug would have been absorbed by this time.","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Gastrointestinal","topicOriginal":"Gastrointestinal","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Gastrointestinal","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":["whitespace/page_marker_normalization"]},{"id":"q-524fbe6f7bb2","stem":"Ans. (c) Primary pulmonary hypertension (Ref: KDT 6/e p180, 182) PGI2 analogs (like epoprostenol and treprostinil) are used for treatment of pulmonary hypertension. PGE1 analogs are used for first trimester abortion (misoprostol), impotence (alprostadil) and maintenance of the patency of ductus arteriosus (alprostadil).","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Cardiovascular","topicOriginal":"Cardiovascular","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Cardiovascular","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-8ca1c2ddfce3","stem":"Ans. (a) Tinnitus is an early symptom; (b) 10-30 g causes poisoning (Ref: KDT 6/e p189-190; Washington Mannual of Medical Therapeutics, 31/e p586) • Symptoms of aspirin poisoning –– At anti-inflammatory dose (3-6 gm/day) produces the syndrome called Salicylism comprising of dizziness, tin- nitus, vertigo, excitement, mental confusion, reversible impairment of hearing. –– The dose has to be titrated to one which is just below that produces these symptoms, tinnitus is a good guide. –– Metabolic acidosis, hyperthermia and acidotic breathing are seen in severe poisoning –– Aspirin causes platelet dysfunction by inhibiting cyclooxygenase pathway. –– Fatal dose in adult is 15-30 g but considerably lower in children.","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-ce9bf1a80555","stem":"Ans. (a) Aspirin; (b) Indomethacin; (e) Diclofenac (Ref: KDT 6/e p187, 193, 195) • Aspirin inhibits COX irreversibly by acetylating one of its serine residues; return of COX activity depends on syn- thesis of fresh enzyme. Other NSAIDS like indomethacin, diclofenac etc are competitive and reversible inhibitors of COX, return of activity depends on their dissociation from the enzyme which is turn is governed by the pharmacoki- netic characteristics of the compound. • Corticosteroids like betamethasone act by inhibiting phospholipase A2. https://kat.cr/user/Blink99/ Review of Pharmacology 142 Autacoids","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic","url_or_navigation_embedded"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-85469f7934a7","stem":"Ans. (d) Prednisolone (Ref: KDT 6/e p279) It is a powerful anti-inflammatory agent. It acts by inhibiting the synthesis of PG, TX and LT via the inhibition of phospho- lipase A2 enzyme. However, its most important mechanism of anti-inflammatory action is the inhibition of chemotaxis.","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-b0c094269d0f","stem":"Ans. (a) Treatment of patent ductus arteriosus (Ref: KDT 6/e p181, 191) • PGE2 analogues are used to maintain the patency of ductus arteriosus whereas aspirin or indomethacin are used for the treatment of PDA. • PGE2 is a bronchodilator and can be used to treat bronchial asthma via inhalational route. • Cervical priming and NSAID induced PUD are other indications of this agent.","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Respiratory","topicOriginal":"Respiratory","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Respiratory","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-60a3df5e4b46","stem":"Ans. (a) Leukotrienes (See below) Aspirin inhibits cox enzyme and results in the diversion of AA pathway towards LT synthesis. As LTs are powerful bron­ choconstrictor agents, these may result in the shortness of breath in patients who are susceptible. Aspirin acetylated cox starts producing lipoxins (known as aspirin triggered lipoxins) that also have bronchoconstrictor properties.","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Respiratory","topicOriginal":"Respiratory","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Respiratory","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-dd1bafedd596","stem":"Ans. (d) Treatment with acetylcysteine (Ref: KDT 6/e p189, 190) This patient is a case of salicylate poisoning. Gastric lavage is done to remove unabsorbed poison. Metabolic acido- sis must be corrected by sodium bicarbonate. Alkalization of urine will increase the excretion of this weakly acidic drug. Treatment with acetylcysteine has no role in salicylate poisoning. It is the antidote of choice for paracetamol poisoning.","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Gastrointestinal","topicOriginal":"Gastrointestinal","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Gastrointestinal","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-3a9bc1632956","stem":"Ans. (c) Alprostadil (Ref: KDT 6/e p182) Prostaglandins (like alprostadil) are used to keep ductus arteriosus patent whereas aspirin or indomethacin are used for the treatment (closure) of PDA.","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-e472b9254315","stem":"Ans. (c) Uricosuria (Ref: KDT 6/e p188) https://kat.cr/user/Blink99/ Review of Pharmacology 144 Autacoids Inhibition of PG synthesis is responsible for analgesic, antipyretic, anti-inflammatory and antiplatelet actions of aspirin. This action is also utilized in the treatment of PDA. High doses of aspirin cause uricosuria whereas therapeutic doses result in hyperuricemia. These effects are unrelated to its action on PG synthesis.","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic","url_or_navigation_embedded"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-4f5035cf963f","stem":"Ans. (c) Sulfinpyrazone (Ref: KDT 6/e p205) This drug is uricosuric agent and is used in the treatment of hyperuricemia. https://kat.cr/user/Blink99/ Autacoids 145 145 General Pharmacology Autacoids","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic","url_or_navigation_embedded"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-870c673bccc5","stem":"Ans. (b) Indomethacin (Ref: KDT 6/e p206) NSAIDs except aspirin are the agents of choice for the treatment of acute gout.","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-78955db446c1","stem":"Ans. (d) Etanercept (Ref: KDT 6/e p205) Infliximab and etanercept are TNF-αantagonists useful for the treatment of rheumatoid arthritis. These are administered by parenteral route. TNF-α antagonists can cause reactivation of latent tuberculosis.","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Infectious Diseases","topicOriginal":"Infectious Diseases","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Infectious Diseases","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-7e4ef34817c5","stem":"Ans. (c) 6-Mercaptopurine (Ref: KDT 6/e p209) Allopurinol inhibits xanthine oxidase, which metabolises 6-MP and azathioprine, so dose of 6-MP is reduced to 1⁄4-1⁄2 if used along with allopurinol.","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":["whitespace/page_marker_normalization"]},{"id":"q-8d0a6a2be07e","stem":"Ans. (c) Phenylephrine (Ref: Harrison 17/e p299; KDT 6/e p295-296) • Phenylephrine is an α-agonist and will cause vasoconstriction therefore not used in erectile dysfunction where vaso- dilation of penile vessels is required. • Other drugs given in the option are used for this condition • For further details, see chapter 19.","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-1963922a5905","stem":"Ans. (c) Bosentan (Ref: Katzung 10/e p287) • Bosentan is a non-specific antagonist of endothelin receptors (both ETA and ETB). It is useful for the treatment of pri- mary pulmonary hypertension. • Omapatrilat is a vasopeptidase inhibitor used for the treatment of CHF.","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Cardiovascular","topicOriginal":"Cardiovascular","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Cardiovascular","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-52b948a1bf3f","stem":"Ans. (b). Colchicine (Ref: KK Sharma 2/e p356) This patient’s acute onset pain and swelling of the right first metatarsophalangeal joint and joint aspirate showing needle- shaped negatively birefringent crystals indicate that he most likely has acute gouty arthritis. Colchicine is an effective anti- inflammatory agent in acute gouty arthritis but commonly result in nausea, vomiting, diarrhea and myopathy as adverse effects.","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-bdf140e5b9d2","stem":"Ans (d) Palliative treatment of patent ductus arteriosus (Ref. KDT 7th/189-190)","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-fb56d9ba067e","stem":"Ans. (a) Naproxen (Ref: KDT 7/e p215, 217) • NSAIDs like naproxen have no role in chronic gout • Uricosuric drugs like probenecid and sulfinpyrazone are ineffective in the presence of renal insufficiency • Allopurinol is drug of choice for most cases of chronic gout.","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Renal and Urinary","topicOriginal":"Renal and Urinary","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Renal and Urinary","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-86c22a44f6be","stem":"Ans. (c) Maintenance of a wakeful state (Ref: KDT 7/e p162)","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-10293730c2ab","stem":"it resist the valgus stresses across the knee joint especially when knee is extended.","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Lower Limb","topicOriginal":"Lower Limb","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Lower Limb","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-f749fa1b45b4","stem":"It restricts pure anterior displacement of the tibia when the primary restraint of the anterior cruciate ligament is absent.","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Lower Limb · Suggested","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":"Lower Limb","options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Lower Limb · Suggested","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-8e106c423745","stem":"1 Which of the following statements best describes the scapula? \u0001 a. It usually overlies the 2nd to 9th ribs. \u0001 b. The spine continues laterally as the coracoid process. \u0001 c. The suprascapular notch is found on its spine. \u0001 d. It provides attachment for both heads of biceps. \u0001 e. Most fractures involve the body.","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Thoracic Wall and Ribs","topicOriginal":"Thoracic Wall and Ribs","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Thoracic Wall and Ribs","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-fa69f388318a","stem":"2 Which statement is the best ending for the following sentence? When considering the humerus, it should be noted that the: \u0001 a. Lesser tubercle has three facets. \u0001 b. Bicipital groove separates the greater and lesser tubercles. \u0001 c. Surgical neck separates the head from the tubercles. \u0001 d. Common extensor origin is the medial epicondyle. \u0001 e. Capitulum articulates with the ulna.","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Upper Limb","topicOriginal":"Upper Limb","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Upper Limb","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-7667f9067bae","stem":"3 Which of the following statements regarding the radius and ulna is correct? \u0001 a. Both have a styloid process at the proximal end. \u0001 b. Both articulate with the humerus at the elbow joint. \u0001 c. Both articulate with the carpal bones at the wrist joint. \u0001 d. Direct injury usually produces transverse fractures of both bones in the distal third. \u0001 e. Fracture is most commonly of the Smith’s type. 1 2 UPPER LIMB: QUESTIONS","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Upper Limb","topicOriginal":"Upper Limb","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Upper Limb","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-0ce6d47e68b1","stem":"4 Which of these statements best describes the carpus? \u0001 a. It is markedly concave from side to side anteriorly. \u0001 b. The most commonly fractured bone is the lunate. \u0001 c. The scaphoid articulates with the 1st metacarpal. \u0001 d. Fracture of the hamate may result in damage to the median nerve. \u0001 e. The pisiform is usually the first bone to begin ossification.","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Thoracic Wall and Ribs","topicOriginal":"Thoracic Wall and Ribs","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Thoracic Wall and Ribs","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-25c325785ab6","stem":"5 Which statement most appropriately describes the female breast? \u0001 a. It overlies the 3rd to 8th ribs. \u0001 b. It consists of 2–3 lobules. \u0001 c. It has suspensory ligaments, which tether the der- mis to the fascia of the chest wall. \u0001 d. The retro-mammary space lies deep to pectoralis major. \u0001 e. The areolar glands are responsible for lactation.","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Thoracic Wall and Ribs","topicOriginal":"Thoracic Wall and Ribs","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Thoracic Wall and Ribs","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-05c9b85b8540","stem":"6 Which of these statements best describes the pectoral gir- dle and shoulder? \u0001 a. The clavicle ossifies in the foetus. \u0001 b. The clavicle most commonly fractures at the junc- tion of the medial and middle third. \u0001 c. The subscapularis bursa is separate from the cap- sule of the shoulder joint. \u0001 d. The capsule of the shoulder joint communicates with the subacromial bursa. \u0001 e. The short head of biceps lies within the capsule of the shoulder joint.","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Thoracic Wall and Ribs","topicOriginal":"Thoracic Wall and Ribs","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Thoracic Wall and Ribs","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-2dded1fdb5d7","stem":"7 Which of the following is true of the rotator cuff? \u0001 a. Teres major is one of its four constituent muscles. \u0001 b. Infraspinatus is innervated by the suprascapular nerve. \u0001 c. Part of its action is to pull the humeral head superiorly. \u0001 d. Subscapularis inserts onto the greater tuberosity of the humerus. \u0001 e. It is deficient anteriorly. 3 UPPER LIMB: QUESTIONS","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Upper Limb","topicOriginal":"Upper Limb","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Upper Limb","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-cd0e7ac80505","stem":"8 When considering the axilla, which of the following statements is accurate? \u0001 a. Pectoralis major and minor contribute to the ante- rior wall. \u0001 b. The long thoracic nerve runs on the posterior wall. \u0001 c. Teres minor forms the lower part of the posterior wall. \u0001 d. The axillary artery lies anterior to the axillary sheath. \u0001 e. The axillary nerve exits through the triangular space.","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Thoracic Wall and Ribs","topicOriginal":"Thoracic Wall and Ribs","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Thoracic Wall and Ribs","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-be935aa3d46f","stem":"9 Which of these sentences best describes the elbow joint? \u0001 a. The capitulum articulates with the head of the ulna. \u0001 b. The capsule of the joint attaches to the radius. \u0001 c. The annular ligament is attached to the ulna, but not to the radius. \u0001 d. The radial nerve is a posterior relation. \u0001 e. The valgus angle created by the joint is larger in men than in women.","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Thoracic Wall and Ribs","topicOriginal":"Thoracic Wall and Ribs","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Thoracic Wall and Ribs","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-c39610604e31","stem":"10 Which statement correctly describes the cubital fossa? \u0001 a. Its borders are entirely muscular. \u0001 b. A branch of the musculocutaneous nerve is found in its floor. \u0001 c. The median cubital vein traverses its roof. \u0001 d. The median nerve lies lateral to the brachial artery within it. \u0001 e. The radial nerve lies medial to the tendon of biceps within it. 4 UPPER LIMB: QUESTIONS","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Thoracic Wall and Ribs","topicOriginal":"Thoracic Wall and Ribs","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Thoracic Wall and Ribs","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-caaf46d08ad7","stem":"11 Which of the following accurately describes the anterior compartment of the forearm? \u0001 a. The median nerve enters the forearm between the two heads of flexor carpi ulnaris. \u0001 b. Palmaris longus is present in approximately 15% of arms. \u0001 c. Flexor digitorum profundus originates from the common flexor origin. \u0001 d. Flexor pollicis longus is characteristically uni- pennate. \u0001 e. The superficial flexor muscles are all innervated by the median nerve.","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Thoracic Wall and Ribs","topicOriginal":"Thoracic Wall and Ribs","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Thoracic Wall and Ribs","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-c86231c9931e","stem":"12 Which of the following best describes the forearm? \u0001 a. The median nerve is related anteriorly to flexor digitorum superficialis. \u0001 b. Pronator quadratus lies deep to the long flexor tendons just proximal to the wrist. \u0001 c. The ulnar nerve lies superficial to flexor carpi ulnaris. \u0001 d. Flexor digitorum profundus is partially supplied by the posterior interosseous nerve. \u0001 e. The radial nerve carries motor fibres in the anter- ior compartment of the forearm.","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Thoracic Wall and Ribs","topicOriginal":"Thoracic Wall and Ribs","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Thoracic Wall and Ribs","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-aeda8177d14f","stem":"13 Which of the following statements best describes the structures in the posterior compartment of the forearm? \u0001 a. Brachioradialis is innervated by the posterior interosseous nerve. \u0001 b. The common extensor origin is the medial epi- condyle of the humerus. \u0001 c. The posterior interosseous nerve pierces supinator. \u0001 d. The extensor retinaculum is attached to the radius and the ulna. \u0001 e. The extensor tendons pass through the extensor retinaculum in the same fascial compartment. 5 UPPER LIMB: QUESTIONS","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Thoracic Wall and Ribs","topicOriginal":"Thoracic Wall and Ribs","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Thoracic Wall and Ribs","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-d8353c45ec63","stem":"14 Which of these statements about the wrist joint is correct? \u0001 a. It is a synovial joint between the ulna and the carpal bones. \u0001 b. The triquetral articulates with the ulna. \u0001 c. The joint line lies at the level of the styloid pro- cess of the ulna. \u0001 d. It has palmar, dorsal and collateral ligaments. \u0001 e. The capsule is continuous with the distal radio- ulnar joint.","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Upper Limb","topicOriginal":"Upper Limb","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Upper Limb","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-b116b48cfe9f","stem":"15 Which of the following statements best describes the carpal tunnel? \u0001 a. The flexor retinaculum is attached to the trapezoid. \u0001 b. The tendons of flexor digitorum superficialis lie with the index and little finger tendons anterior to those of the middle and ring fingers. \u0001 c. The median nerve lies directly deep to the ten- dons of flexor digitorum superficialis. \u0001 d. The palmar cutaneous branch of the median nerve lies superficial to the flexor retinaculum. \u0001 e. The tendon of flexor carpi ulnaris lies within the carpel tunnel.","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Thoracic Wall and Ribs","topicOriginal":"Thoracic Wall and Ribs","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Thoracic Wall and Ribs","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-f28315572643","stem":"16 The flexor tendons of the forearm run within sheaths in the hand. Which of the following statements best describes these structures? \u0001 a. The fibrous flexor sheaths run from the metacar- pal heads to the proximal phalanges. \u0001 b. The annular pullies overlie the interphalangeal joints. \u0001 c. The synovial sheath of the middle finger tendon is continuous with its sheath in the carpal tunnel. \u0001 d. The cruciform pullies overlie the phalanges. \u0001 e. The flexor digitorum profundus tendon pierces the flexor digitorum superficialis tendon. 6 UPPER LIMB: QUESTIONS","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Thoracic Wall and Ribs","topicOriginal":"Thoracic Wall and Ribs","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Thoracic Wall and Ribs","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-89b233ac8b70","stem":"17 Which statement regarding the anatomical snuffbox is correct? \u0001 a. Abductor pollicis longus and extensor pollicis brevis form the ulnar border. \u0001 b. The extensor pollicis longus tendon forms the radial border. \u0001 c. The floor is formed by the scaphoid and trapezoid. \u0001 d. The radial artery runs in the floor. \u0001 e. The origin of the basilic vein is found in its roof.","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Upper Limb · Suggested","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":"Upper Limb","options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Upper Limb · Suggested","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-d2bfe7d93c6c","stem":"18 Which statement regarding the anatomical spaces of the hand is true? \u0001 a. The thenar space lies lateral to the 3rd metacarpal. \u0001 b. The hypothenar space lies lateral to the 5th metacarpal. \u0001 c. The midpalmar space lies superficial to the pal- mar aponeurosis. \u0001 d. The pulp spaces lie on the dorsal aspect of the fingers and thumb. \u0001 e. The web spaces lie between adjacent metacarpal bones.","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Upper Limb · Suggested","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":"Upper Limb","options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Upper Limb · Suggested","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-2502e6e7b6b3","stem":"19 Which of the following best describes the lumbricals? \u0001 a. The lumbricals arise from the tendons of flexor digitorum superficialis. \u0001 b. There is a lumbrical inserted into the extensor expansion of all five digits. \u0001 c. The median and radial nerves innervate the lumbricals. \u0001 d. The action of the lumbricals is to extend the dis- tal interphalangeal joint. \u0001 e. The action of the lumbricals is to flex the proximal interphalangeal joint. 7 UPPER LIMB: QUESTIONS","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Thoracic Wall and Ribs","topicOriginal":"Thoracic Wall and Ribs","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Thoracic Wall and Ribs","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-a47d23761326","stem":"20 The following are statements about the interosseous muscles of the hand. Which is correct? \u0001 a. The palmar interossei abduct the fingers. \u0001 b. There are four palmar interossei. \u0001 c. There are three dorsal interossei. \u0001 d. The dorsal interossei are bipennate. \u0001 e. The palmar interossei are all supplied by the median nerve.","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Upper Limb · Suggested","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":"Upper Limb","options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Upper Limb · Suggested","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-0a2069bb8735","stem":"21 These statements consider the vasculature and lymph- atic drainage of the breast. Which is true? \u0001 a. The main blood supply is derived from branches of the internal mammary artery. \u0001 b. Venous drainage is predominantly to the internal mammary vein. \u0001 c. Lymphatic drainage is divided evenly between the axillary and internal mammary nodes. \u0001 d. The superficial lymphatics of each breast remain separate in healthy people. \u0001 e. All the axillary lymphatics drain through the apical axillary nodes.","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-33bc2012925d","stem":"22 Which of the following statements best describes the subclavian artery? \u0001 a. It is divided into three parts by the scalenus medius muscle. \u0001 b. The second part gives no constant branch. \u0001 c. It is crossed posteriorly by the vagus nerve. \u0001 d. It lies anterior to the subclavian vein. \u0001 e. It is prone to aneurysmal change in the presence of a cervical rib. 8 UPPER LIMB: QUESTIONS","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Thoracic Wall and Ribs","topicOriginal":"Thoracic Wall and Ribs","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Thoracic Wall and Ribs","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-78f91c43f92b","stem":"23 Which of the following statements about the axillary artery is accurate? \u0001 a. It is divided into three parts by pectoralis major. \u0001 b. It becomes the brachial artery at the inferior bor- der of teres minor. \u0001 c. The cords of the brachial plexus are named accord- ing to their positions relative to the first part. \u0001 d. The axillary vein lies medial throughout its course. \u0001 e. It gives the suprascapular artery as one of its branches.","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Upper Limb","topicOriginal":"Upper Limb","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Upper Limb","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-b832b6d348dc","stem":"24 The brachial plexus gives rise to the nerve supply of the upper limb. Which of these statements about its branches is correct? \u0001 a. The terminal branch of the medial cord is the ulnar nerve. \u0001 b. The terminal branch of the posterior cord is the median nerve. \u0001 c. The axillary nerve is a branch of the lateral cord. \u0001 d. The radial nerve arises from the medial cord. \u0001 e. The long thoracic nerve arises from the roots.","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Upper Limb","topicOriginal":"Upper Limb","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Upper Limb","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-d9a3cd859793","stem":"25 Which of these statements about the musculocutaneous nerve is correct? \u0001 a. It is a branch of the medial cord of the brachial plexus. \u0001 b. It pierces brachialis to enter the anterior compart- ment of the arm. \u0001 c. Brachioradialis is innervated by one of its branches. \u0001 d. The medial cutaneous nerve of the forearm is its terminal branch. \u0001 e. It contributes to the innervation of the shoulder joint. 9 UPPER LIMB: QUESTIONS","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Thoracic Wall and Ribs","topicOriginal":"Thoracic Wall and Ribs","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Thoracic Wall and Ribs","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-856d3c90bc14","stem":"26 Select the most appropriate ending for the following sentence. The median nerve: \u0001 a. Arises from both the posterior and medial cords of the brachial plexus. \u0001 b. Passes from the medial to the lateral side of the brachial artery in the arm. \u0001 c. Gives no muscular branches in the arm. \u0001 d. Enters the forearm by passing between the two heads of flexor carpi ulnaris. \u0001 e. Supplies all of flexor digitorum profundus.","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Upper Limb","topicOriginal":"Upper Limb","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Upper Limb","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-2f0ee54425ea","stem":"27 Which of these statements best completes the following sentence? The ulnar nerve: \u0001 a. Has the ulnar artery as a medial relation at the wrist. \u0001 b. Passes deep to the flexor retinaculum at the wrist. \u0001 c. Usually innervates the lateral two lumbricals. \u0001 d. Innervates adductor pollicis. \u0001 e. Innervates the skin of the lateral (radial) 11⁄2 digits.","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Upper Limb","topicOriginal":"Upper Limb","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Upper Limb","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":["whitespace/page_marker_normalization"]},{"id":"q-ad842e7ecc81","stem":"28 Which statement considering the relations of nerves to the humerus is the most accurate? \u0001 a. The axillary nerve runs around the anatomical neck. \u0001 b. The median nerve runs in the spiral groove. \u0001 c. Mid-shaft humeral fractures will usually result in complete paralysis of triceps. \u0001 d. The ulnar nerve is related to the lateral epicondyle. \u0001 e. Deltoid may atrophy following shoulder dislocation. 10 UPPER LIMB: QUESTIONS","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Upper Limb","topicOriginal":"Upper Limb","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Upper Limb","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-c0ba9a2aa2e6","stem":"29 Consider the dermatomes of the upper limb. Which of the following statements best completes this sentence? The skin over the: \u0001 a. Clavicle derives its cutaneous innervation from C4. \u0001 b. Thumb derives its cutaneous innervation from T1. \u0001 c. Little finger derives its cutaneous innervation from C7. \u0001 d. Posterior surface of the forearm derives its cuta- neous innervation from T1. \u0001 e. Lateral aspect of the upper limb derives its cuta- neous innervation from C8 and T1.","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Upper Limb · Suggested","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":"Upper Limb","options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Upper Limb · Suggested","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-e06f5ee2db12","stem":"30 Which of the following statements ends this sentence correctly? The spinal nerve root mediating the: \u0001 a. Biceps jerk is C5. \u0001 b. Supinator jerk is C7. \u0001 c. Triceps jerk is C6. \u0001 d. Deltoid reflex is C4. \u0001 e. Pectoral reflex is C5. UPPER LIMB: ANSWERS 11 ANSWERS","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-8412e3aecaa2","stem":"1 Which of these statements about the femur is incorrect? \u0001 a. The intertrochanteric line lies on the anterior aspect of the bone. \u0001 b. The lateral condyle projects more anteriorly than the medial condyle. \u0001 c. Gluteus minimus is attached to the lesser trochanter. \u0001 d. The intertrochanteric crest is the insertion point for the quadratus femoris. \u0001 e. Gluteus maximus attaches to the gluteal tuberosity.","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Lower Limb","topicOriginal":"Lower Limb","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Lower Limb","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-905b2fb2151b","stem":"2 Which of these statements about the tibia and fibula is correct? \u0001 a. The medial tibial condyle is smaller and more cir- cular than the lateral. \u0001 b. Sartorius, gracilis and semitendinosus insert into the tibia. \u0001 c. Popliteus arises below the soleal line. \u0001 d. The common fibular nerve is subcutaneous at the neck of the fibula. \u0001 e. The fibularis muscles arise from the anterior sur- face of the fibula. 27 LOWER LIMB: QUESTIONS 28 LOWER LIMB: QUESTIONS","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Head and Neck · Suggested","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":"Head and Neck","options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Head and Neck · Suggested","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-1b8512a95d35","stem":"3 Which statement about the arches of the foot is not correct? \u0001 a. The medial arch contains the medial cuneiform as one of its bony elements. \u0001 b. The lateral arch contains the lateral two metatar- sals as bony elements. \u0001 c. Flexor hallucis longus is important in maintaining the stability of the lateral arch. \u0001 d. Fibularis longus helps to maintain the lateral arch. \u0001 e. Fibularis longus helps to maintain the transverse arch.","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Lower Limb · Suggested","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":"Lower Limb","options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Lower Limb · Suggested","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-d1d0db4c7797","stem":"4 Which of the following best describes the anatomy of the gluteal muscles? \u0001 a. Gluteus maximus is supplied by the superior glu- teal artery. \u0001 b. Gluteus maximus inserts into the greater trochanter. \u0001 c. Gluteus medius is a lateral rotator at the hip joint. \u0001 d. They all take origin from the ilium. \u0001 e. Gluteus minimus is innervated by the inferior glu- teal nerve.","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Thoracic Wall and Ribs","topicOriginal":"Thoracic Wall and Ribs","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Thoracic Wall and Ribs","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-347439ed1ec4","stem":"5 Which of the following statements incorrectly describes the relations of the hip joint? \u0001 a. Rectus femoris is a superior relation. \u0001 b. Obturator externus is an inferior relation. \u0001 c. The iliotibial tract is a lateral relation. \u0001 d. The femoral vein is an anterior relation. \u0001 e. Pectineus is a lateral relation.","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Thoracic Wall and Ribs","topicOriginal":"Thoracic Wall and Ribs","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Thoracic Wall and Ribs","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-948993903929","stem":"6 Consider the fascia and compartments of the thigh, Which statement is correct? \u0001 a. The iliotibial tract lies superficial to the fascia lata. \u0001 b. The iliotibial tract inserts onto the lateral epi- condyle of the femur. \u0001 c. The femoral vein passes through the fascia lata. \u0001 d. There are three compartments. \u0001 e. There are three intermuscular septa in the thigh. 29","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Lower Limb","topicOriginal":"Lower Limb","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Lower Limb","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-d539c7373498","stem":"7 Consider these statements about the anterior compart- ment of the thigh, Which of them is not correct? \u0001 a. All the muscles of the anterior compartment are innervated by the femoral nerve. \u0001 b. Sartorius flexes both the hip and knee joint. \u0001 c. The quadriceps tendon inserts directly onto the tibia. \u0001 d. Quadriceps femoris flexes the hip joint. \u0001 e. Vastus medialis is of importance in preventing lat- eral dislocation of the patella.","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Lower Limb","topicOriginal":"Lower Limb","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Lower Limb","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-5ec72ba3d05d","stem":"8 Which statement best describes the medial compartment of the thigh? \u0001 a. Adductor magnus, longus and brevis insert onto the linea aspera. \u0001 b. All the muscles of the medial compartment are innervated by the obturator nerve. \u0001 c. All the muscles of the medial compartment take origin from the ischium. \u0001 d. The obturator nerve is split into anterior and pos- terior divisions by adductor longus. \u0001 e. All the muscles of the medial compartment insert onto the femur.","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Thoracic Wall and Ribs","topicOriginal":"Thoracic Wall and Ribs","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Thoracic Wall and Ribs","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-3faeae47d74f","stem":"9 Which statement best describes the posterior compart- ment of the thigh? \u0001 a. The common origin for the posterior thigh muscles (hamstrings) is the ischial spine. \u0001 b. Adductor magnus is a muscle of the posterior compartment. \u0001 c. All the hamstring muscles are innervated by the obturator nerve. \u0001 d. Both heads of biceps femoris take origin from the pelvis. \u0001 e. Most of the skin overlying the posterior aspect of the thigh is innervated by the sciatic nerve. LOWER LIMB: QUESTIONS 30 LOWER LIMB: QUESTIONS","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Thoracic Wall and Ribs","topicOriginal":"Thoracic Wall and Ribs","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Thoracic Wall and Ribs","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-ecaa0d2c0fbe","stem":"10 Consider the femoral sheath and canal, Which of these statements is not correct? \u0001 a. The femoral sheath is continuous with the trans- versalis fascia. \u0001 b. The sheath contains the femoral nerve. \u0001 c. The femoral canal lies medial within the sheath. \u0001 d. The canal is wider in the female. \u0001 e. The canal contains Cloquet’s node.","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Lower Limb · Suggested","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":"Lower Limb","options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Lower Limb · Suggested","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-fdb3b6bdf3a9","stem":"11 Which statement correctly describes the femoral triangle? \u0001 a. Its medial boundary is the lateral border of adductor longus. \u0001 b. The floor is formed by the fascia lata. \u0001 c. The femoral vein passes to lie anterior to the femoral artery at the apex. \u0001 d. It contains the short saphenous vein. \u0001 e. It contains the saphenous nerve.","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Thoracic Wall and Ribs","topicOriginal":"Thoracic Wall and Ribs","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Thoracic Wall and Ribs","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-89bf75708932","stem":"12 Which of these statements about the adductor canal is incorrect? \u0001 a. It is the communication between the femoral tri- angle and popliteal fossa. \u0001 b. It is bordered posteriorly by adductor longus and magnus. \u0001 c. It contains the nerve to vastus medialis. \u0001 d. The saphenous nerve exits the canal medially. \u0001 e. The femoral artery exits through a hiatus in adductor longus. 31 LOWER LIMB: QUESTIONS","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Diaphragm","topicOriginal":"Diaphragm","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Diaphragm","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-c96a38e9a450","stem":"13 Which statement does not correctly describe the liga- ments of the knee joint? \u0001 a. The medial meniscus is larger than the lateral meniscus. \u0001 b. The lateral meniscus is attached to the lateral col- lateral ligament. \u0001 c. The transverse ligament of the knee attaches to both menisci. \u0001 d. The anterior cruciate ligament prevents hyper- extension of the knee. \u0001 e. The posterior cruciate ligament inserts onto the medial femoral condyle.","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Thoracic Wall and Ribs","topicOriginal":"Thoracic Wall and Ribs","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Thoracic Wall and Ribs","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-8826aa709553","stem":"14 Which of the following statements correctly describes the capsule of the knee joint? \u0001 a. It is thickest anteriorly. \u0001 b. The suprapatellar bursa communicates with the knee joint. \u0001 c. Both the cruciate ligaments lie within the syn- ovial cavity. \u0001 d. The infrapatellar fat pad contains the subcutane- ous infrapatellar bursa. \u0001 e. The posterior meniscofemoral ligament arises from the medial meniscus.","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Thoracic Wall and Ribs","topicOriginal":"Thoracic Wall and Ribs","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Thoracic Wall and Ribs","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-7d9c7c652a1d","stem":"15 Which statement about the popliteal fossa is not true? \u0001 a. Is bounded superolaterally by biceps femoris. \u0001 b. Contains the popliteal artery as the deepest structure. \u0001 c. Usually contains the bifurcation of the sciatic nerve. \u0001 d. Contains the long saphenous vein in its roof. \u0001 e. Contains the formation of the sural nerve. 32 LOWER LIMB: QUESTIONS","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Lower Limb · Suggested","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":"Lower Limb","options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Lower Limb · Suggested","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-e4277607cdff","stem":"16 Which statement correctly describes the anterior com- partment of the leg? \u0001 a. It is separated from the posterior compartment by the anterior intermuscular septum. \u0001 b. All the muscles of the compartment are innerv- ated by the deep peroneal nerve. \u0001 c. The anterior tibial artery arises in the anterior compartment. \u0001 d. It contains extensor digitorum brevis. \u0001 e. Tibialis anterior assists in eversion of the foot.","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Thoracic Wall and Ribs","topicOriginal":"Thoracic Wall and Ribs","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Thoracic Wall and Ribs","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-4b85e1a60afb","stem":"17 Consider the lateral compartment of the leg. Which statement is correct? \u0001 a. All the muscles of the lateral compartment are innervated by the superficial peroneal nerve. \u0001 b. It contains the peroneal artery. \u0001 c. It contains peroneus longus, brevis and tertius. \u0001 d. The peroneus longus tendon grooves the lateral malleolus. \u0001 e. The muscles of the lateral compartment princi- pally invert the foot.","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Lower Limb · Suggested","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":"Lower Limb","options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Lower Limb · Suggested","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-75e83e4df3aa","stem":"18 Which statement regarding the tendons of the muscles of the lateral compartment of the leg is incorrect? \u0001 a. The peroneus longus tendon runs posterior to that of peroneus brevis at the lateral malleolus. \u0001 b. Both tendons pass under the superior and infer- ior peroneal retinaculae. \u0001 c. They share a common synovial sheath as they pass under both retinaculae. \u0001 d. The tendons are separated by the peroneal trochlea. \u0001 e. The peroneus longus tendon passes deep to the long plantar ligament. 33 LOWER LIMB: QUESTIONS","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-68d8bc875ece","stem":"19 Which of the following does not correctly describe the posterior compartment of the leg? \u0001 a. The muscles are separated into deep and superfi- cial groups by the posterior intermuscular septum. \u0001 b. All of the muscles are innervated by the tibial nerve. \u0001 c. It contains both the anterior and posterior tibial arteries. \u0001 d. Flexor hallucis longus takes origin from the fib- ula, lateral to flexor digitorum longus. \u0001 e. Popliteus unlocks the knee by laterally rotating the femur.","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Thoracic Wall and Ribs","topicOriginal":"Thoracic Wall and Ribs","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Thoracic Wall and Ribs","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-7aa161dd4664","stem":"20 Which statement, regarding the arterial anastomosis around the hip joint is incorrect? \u0001 a. The circumflex femoral arteries usually arise from the profunda femoris. \u0001 b. The artery to the head of the femur is a branch of the femoral artery. \u0001 c. The artery to the head of the femur enters with the ligamentum teres. \u0001 d. The hip joint is supplied by retinacular branches. \u0001 e. Avascular necrosis is common in fractures of the femoral neck.","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Lower Limb","topicOriginal":"Lower Limb","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Lower Limb","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-7a743dc55f3c","stem":"21 Which statement best describes the common femoral artery? \u0001 a. Commences at the midpoint of the inguinal ligament. \u0001 b. Gives the profunda femoris branch within the femoral sheath. \u0001 c. Lies between the saphenous nerve and femoral vein at all levels of the thigh. \u0001 d. Lies over pectineus as it passes under the inguinal ligament. \u0001 e. Becomes the popliteal artery as it leaves the fem- oral triangle. 34 LOWER LIMB: QUESTIONS","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Thoracic Wall and Ribs","topicOriginal":"Thoracic Wall and Ribs","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Thoracic Wall and Ribs","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-bddd36b4e149","stem":"22 Which of the following associations of lower limb move- ments and root values is not correct? \u0001 a. Hip flexion \u0001 L3, L4 \u0001 b. Knee flexion \u0001 L5, S1 \u0001 c. Ankle dorsiflexion \u0001 L4, L5 \u0001 d. Inversion \u0001 L4 \u0001 e. Eversion \u0001 L5, S1 LOWER LIMB: ANSWERS 35 ANSWERS","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Lower Limb","topicOriginal":"Lower Limb","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Lower Limb","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-34172cab79fd","stem":"5 cm below the tendinous arch in soleus, and descends along the medial crest of the fibula between tibialis poster- ior and flexor hallucis longus. While it gives perforating branches which supply the lateral compartment structures, it remains in the posterior compartment. LOWER LIMB: ANSWERS 45","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Lower Limb · Suggested","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":"Lower Limb","options":[{"label":"C","text":"Peroneus tertius is a muscle of the anterior compartment."},{"label":"D","text":"The peroneus brevis tendon lies anterior to the peroneus longus tendon throughout, and as such lies in contact with the lateral malleolus which it grooves. The tendon of per- oneus longus does not come into contact with the lateral malleolus."},{"label":"E","text":"They act to evert the foot and are also weak plantarflex- ors. They have a notable role in plantarflexion if the tib- ial nerve is damaged. Peroneus longus also contributes to maintaining the arches of the foot."}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Lower Limb · Suggested","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["repeated_question_stem","topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[],"duplicateStemGroup":"430a1ffd9ff749648430167e56659e9218628b74"},{"id":"q-26b9b3bb4a7c","stem":"1 Which of the following statements about the surface anatomy of the chest is correct? \u0001 a. The manubrium overlies the aortic arch at the level of T4–T5. \u0001 b. The body of the sternum overlies the heart at the level of T5–T8. \u0001 c. The most prominent vertebral spinous process encountered when palpating the spine is that of C6. \u0001 d. The manubriosternal joint is at the level of the junction of T3 and T4. \u0001 e. The xiphisternal joint is at the level of T8.","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Cardiovascular Anatomy","topicOriginal":"Cardiovascular Anatomy","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Cardiovascular Anatomy","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-82faf5c4efce","stem":"2 Consider the surface markings of the pleura, which of the following is correct? \u0001 a. The right and left pleura come into contact in the midline at the level of the jugular notch (supraster- nal notch). \u0001 b. The apex of the pleura is approximately level with the clavicle. \u0001 c. The right pleura passes across the 10th rib in the anterior axillary line. \u0001 d. The left pleura arches laterally at the level of the 4th costal cartilage. \u0001 e. The pleura terminates above the level of the 12th rib. 49 50 THORAX: QUESTIONS","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Thoracic Wall and Ribs","topicOriginal":"Thoracic Wall and Ribs","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Thoracic Wall and Ribs","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-8a47a8d06a58","stem":"3 Which statement about the ribs is accurate? \u0001 a. The head of a typical rib articulates with the trans- verse costal facet of its own vertebra. \u0001 b. The 8th, 9th and 10th ribs are false ribs. \u0001 c. The 1st rib has the subclavian artery as an inferior relation. \u0001 d. The costotransverse ligaments pass from the trans- verse process of the vertebra to the head of the rib. \u0001 e. The intercostal neurovascular bundle has the inter- costal vein as the most inferior structure.","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Thoracic Wall and Ribs","topicOriginal":"Thoracic Wall and Ribs","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Thoracic Wall and Ribs","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-c3ba18120b60","stem":"4 Which of the following statements correctly describes the arterial supply of the body wall? \u0001 a. The internal thoracic artery supplies the anterior body wall as far down as the umbilicus. \u0001 b. The first nine anterior intercostal arteries are direct branches of the internal thoracic artery. \u0001 c. The posterior intercostal arteries are all branches of the thoracic aorta. \u0001 d. The lower anterior abdominal wall is supplied by a branch of the internal iliac artery. \u0001 e. The intercostal veins lie directly superior to the intercostal nerves.","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Thoracic Wall and Ribs","topicOriginal":"Thoracic Wall and Ribs","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Thoracic Wall and Ribs","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-d166c12ea3dc","stem":"5 Which statement regarding the internal thoracic artery is correct? \u0001 a. It is a branch of the aorta. \u0001 b. It arises from the brachiocephalic artery on the right side. \u0001 c. It lies between the external and internal intercos- tal muscles. \u0001 d. It contributes to the blood supply of the breast. \u0001 e. The pericardiophrenic artery is a terminal branch. 51 THORAX: QUESTIONS","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Cardiovascular Anatomy","topicOriginal":"Cardiovascular Anatomy","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Cardiovascular Anatomy","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-ddfde60ac6b0","stem":"6 Which of the following statements best describes the lungs? \u0001 a. Both main bronchi enter the roots of their respect- ive lungs at the level of T6. \u0001 b. The left main bronchus gives its upper lobe bron- chus before joining the lung. \u0001 c. The blood supply of the lung is derived from the pulmonary arteries. \u0001 d. The left lung has a lingular segment. \u0001 e. Blood from the lungs drains directly into the bra- chiocephalic trunks.","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Thoracic Wall and Ribs","topicOriginal":"Thoracic Wall and Ribs","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Thoracic Wall and Ribs","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-aeb23887e353","stem":"7 Which of the following structures does not enter the hilum of the left lung? \u0001 a. The left upper lobe bronchus. \u0001 b. The left pulmonary artery. \u0001 c. The bronchial artery. \u0001 d. Branches of the pulmonary plexus. \u0001 e. Lymphatics which drain into the bronchopulmo- nary lymph nodes.","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Lungs and Pleura","topicOriginal":"Lungs and Pleura","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Lungs and Pleura","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-fc2ff76ed4c4","stem":"8 Which of the following statements regarding the divisions of the mediastinum is true? \u0001 a. The superior mediastinum lies above the level of the T3/T4 intervertebral disc. \u0001 b. The inferior mediastinum is conventionally divided into three parts. \u0001 c. The descending thoracic aorta is entirely in the middle mediastinum. \u0001 d. The pericardial sac and its contents form the anter- ior mediastinum. \u0001 e. The phrenic nerves run in the anterior compartment. 52 THORAX: QUESTIONS","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Cardiovascular Anatomy","topicOriginal":"Cardiovascular Anatomy","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Cardiovascular Anatomy","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-b584e6419754","stem":"9 Which of these statements regarding the superior media- stinum is correct? \u0001 a. The right brachiocephalic vein is shorter than the left. \u0001 b. The vagus nerve enters the thorax posterior to the subclavian artery. \u0001 c. The ligamentum arteriosum usually passes from the left pulmonary artery to the ascending aorta. \u0001 d. The thoracic duct passes across the oesophagus within the superior mediastinum. \u0001 e. The left phrenic nerve passes medial to the aortic arch.","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Diaphragm","topicOriginal":"Diaphragm","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Diaphragm","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-96335ba45f5f","stem":"10 Which of the following statements regarding the trachea is correct? \u0001 a. It commences at the level of C8. \u0001 b. It is in contact with the left vagus nerve in the thorax. \u0001 c. It has a blood supply from the superior thyroid arteries. \u0001 d. It is lined by mucus secreting cells. \u0001 e. It bifurcates at the level of the T2/T3 interverte- bral disc.","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Lungs and Pleura","topicOriginal":"Lungs and Pleura","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Lungs and Pleura","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-d475db1dc11e","stem":"11 Which of the following statements about the oesopha- gus is correct? \u0001 a. Is crossed anteriorly by the left main bronchus. \u0001 b. Passes through the diaphragm with the left phrenic nerve. \u0001 c. Has a blood supply from the superior thyroid artery. \u0001 d. Has a venous drainage into the right gastric vein. \u0001 e. Has smooth muscle in the lower 2/3 of its wall. 53 THORAX: QUESTIONS","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Diaphragm","topicOriginal":"Diaphragm","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Diaphragm","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-ffd6629798a5","stem":"12 Which statement regarding the superior vena cava is correct? \u0001 a. It is formed by the union of the right and left subclavian veins. \u0001 b. The azygos vein drains into its posterior aspect. \u0001 c. It drains blood from all structures superior to the diaphragm. \u0001 d. It forms the posterior boundary of the oblique pericardial sinus. \u0001 e. Has a rudimentary valve at its opening into the right atrium.","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Cardiovascular Anatomy","topicOriginal":"Cardiovascular Anatomy","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Cardiovascular Anatomy","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-dc8581c3f268","stem":"13 Which of the following statements does not correctly describe the pericardium? \u0001 a. The oblique sinus is bounded by the pulmonary veins and the pulmonary arteries. \u0001 b. The transverse sinus lies between the superior vena cava and left atrium posteriorly, and the arch of aorta and pulmonary trunk anteriorly. \u0001 c. It contains the entire ascending aorta. \u0001 d. It is attached to the central tendon of the diaphragm. \u0001 e. The phrenic nerves are directly related to its lat- eral surface on both sides.","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Cardiovascular Anatomy","topicOriginal":"Cardiovascular Anatomy","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Cardiovascular Anatomy","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-fe30556c43b9","stem":"14 Which of these statements does not correctly describe the phrenic nerve? \u0001 a. It innervates the mediastinal pleura. \u0001 b. It innervates the diaphragm from its superior sur- face downwards. \u0001 c. It passes anterior to the hilum of the lung on the left. \u0001 d. It lies lateral to the superior vena cava on the right. \u0001 e. It has the subclavian artery on its medial side on the left. 54 THORAX: QUESTIONS","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Thoracic Wall and Ribs","topicOriginal":"Thoracic Wall and Ribs","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Thoracic Wall and Ribs","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-47ff3bf97642","stem":"15 Consider these statements about the heart, which one is incorrect? \u0001 a. The right lateral border is formed entirely by the right atrium. \u0001 b. The left atrium is visible when the anterior sur- face of the heart is viewed. \u0001 c. The inferior surface is formed by the left and right ventricles, together with the right atrium. \u0001 d. The base of the heart is directly related to the surface of the diaphragm. \u0001 e. The pulmonary trunk arises anterior to the aorta.","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Cardiovascular Anatomy","topicOriginal":"Cardiovascular Anatomy","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Cardiovascular Anatomy","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-38d6a8fb2681","stem":"16 Which statement about the right atrium is incorrect? \u0001 a. The crista terminalis separates the smooth walled part from the rough walled part. \u0001 b. The opening of the inferior vena cava into the right atrium is valveless. \u0001 c. It contains the embryological remnant of the foramen ovale. \u0001 d. It receives the coronary sinus. \u0001 e. The smooth walled part is derived from the sinus venosus of the primitive heart tube.","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Cardiovascular Anatomy","topicOriginal":"Cardiovascular Anatomy","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Cardiovascular Anatomy","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-c96d3b28b823","stem":"17 Which statement about the left ventricle is correct? \u0001 a. The mitral valve is tricuspid. \u0001 b. The papillary muscles and chordae tendinae anchor the aortic valve. \u0001 c. It lies posterior to the right ventricle. \u0001 d. Its wall has a smooth and a rough portion. \u0001 e. The aortic valve is bicuspid. 55 THORAX: QUESTIONS","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Cardiovascular Anatomy","topicOriginal":"Cardiovascular Anatomy","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Cardiovascular Anatomy","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-142cb0199ce8","stem":"18 Which statement about the conducting system of the heart is correct? \u0001 a. The sinoatrial node lies in the crista terminalis, next to the entrance of the inferior vena cava. \u0001 b. The atrioventricular node lies next to the open- ing of the coronary sinus. \u0001 c. The bundle of His divides in the membranous part of the interventricular septum. \u0001 d. The right bundle branch has anterior and pos- terior divisions. \u0001 e. The sinoatrial node is supplied by the right coro- nary artery in 40% of cases.","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Cardiovascular Anatomy","topicOriginal":"Cardiovascular Anatomy","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Cardiovascular Anatomy","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-cfa84671ce34","stem":"19 Which statement about the valves of the heart is correct? \u0001 a. Both the pulmonary and aortic valves are bicuspid. \u0001 b. The 1st heart sound corresponds to closure of the aortic and pulmonary valves. \u0001 c. Closure of the aortic valve is best heard in the left 2nd intercostal space. \u0001 d. The chorda tendinae tether the atrioventricular valves to pectinate muscle directly. \u0001 e. The chorda tendinae prevent eversion of the atrio- ventricular valves.","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Cardiovascular Anatomy","topicOriginal":"Cardiovascular Anatomy","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Cardiovascular Anatomy","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-a12a8e867aeb","stem":"20 Which statement about the right coronary artery is incorrect? \u0001 a. Supplies the atrioventricular node in 90% of cases. \u0001 b. Usually gives off the posterior interventricular artery. \u0001 c. Contributes to the posterior interventricular artery in a co-dominant circulation. \u0001 d. Anastomoses with the circumflex branch of the left coronary artery. \u0001 e. Arises from the right posterior aortic sinus. 56 THORAX: QUESTIONS","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Cardiovascular Anatomy","topicOriginal":"Cardiovascular Anatomy","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Cardiovascular Anatomy","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-deae25ff311a","stem":"21 Which statement regarding the left coronary artery is incorrect? \u0001 a. Is usually shorter than the right coronary artery. \u0001 b. Has an intermediate branch in 1/3 of cases. \u0001 c. Supplies the sinoatrial node in 40% of cases. \u0001 d. Gives off the posterior interventricular artery in a left dominant circulation. \u0001 e. Passes posterior to the left auricle.","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Cardiovascular Anatomy","topicOriginal":"Cardiovascular Anatomy","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Cardiovascular Anatomy","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-91a171154aba","stem":"22 Which statement incorrectly describes the venous drain- age of the heart? \u0001 a. Venae cordis minimae drain into all four chambers. \u0001 b. The anterior cardiac veins drain into the right atrium. \u0001 c. The coronary sinus lies in the posterior interven- tricular groove. \u0001 d. The middle cardiac vein is a tributary of the cor- onary sinus. \u0001 e. The small cardiac vein is a tributary of the cor- onary sinus.","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Cardiovascular Anatomy","topicOriginal":"Cardiovascular Anatomy","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Cardiovascular Anatomy","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-eeb16cb65d43","stem":"23 Which statement about the primitive aortic arches is correct? \u0001 a. The first arches persist. \u0001 b. The second arches become the carotids. \u0001 c. The third arches disappear. \u0001 d. The fourth arch links up with the descending aorta on the left side. \u0001 e. The fifth arches become the pulmonary arteries. 57 THORAX: QUESTIONS","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Head and Neck","topicOriginal":"Head and Neck","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Head and Neck","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-c7296aa88a55","stem":"24 Which statement regarding the foetal circulation is correct? \u0001 a. The septum secundum lies to the left of the sep- tum primum. \u0001 b. The ductus arteriosus closes at birth. \u0001 c. The foramen ovale is a defect in the septum primum. \u0001 d. The ligamentum teres is the embryological rem- nant of the right umbilical vein. \u0001 e. The umbilical arteries are derived from branches of the external iliac arteries.","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Embryological Development","topicOriginal":"Embryological Development","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Embryological Development","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-8ff5f6764d38","stem":"25 Which statement concerning the azygos system is incorrect? \u0001 a. It comprises veins on either side of the thoracic spine. \u0001 b. The azygos vein passes through the diaphragm with the aorta. \u0001 c. The hemiazygos vein passes through the dia- phragm with the aorta. \u0001 d. The anterior intercostal veins are tributaries of the hemiazygos vein. \u0001 e. The hemiazygos and accessory hemiazygos veins drain into the azygos vein.","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Thoracic Wall and Ribs","topicOriginal":"Thoracic Wall and Ribs","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Thoracic Wall and Ribs","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-0ff7460749aa","stem":"26 Which of the following statements does not correctly describe the thoracic duct? \u0001 a. It originates from the cisterna chyli. \u0001 b. It passes through the diaphragm with the aorta. \u0001 c. It drains all the body tissue below the diaphragm. \u0001 d. It usually drains the entire left side of the body. \u0001 e. It empties into the left subclavian artery. 58 THORAX: QUESTIONS","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Thoracic Wall and Ribs","topicOriginal":"Thoracic Wall and Ribs","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Thoracic Wall and Ribs","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-b165c1681fdc","stem":"27 Which of these statements about the descending aorta is correct? \u0001 a. The descending aorta forms at the level of T6. \u0001 b. The aortic hiatus is at the level of T10. \u0001 c. The aorta usually gives rise to the majority of the posterior intercostal arteries. \u0001 d. Isolated coarctation usually occurs at the level of the hiatus. \u0001 e. Gives rise to a pair of subcostal arteries below the diaphragm.","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Thoracic Wall and Ribs","topicOriginal":"Thoracic Wall and Ribs","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Thoracic Wall and Ribs","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-ccb43f8b59ec","stem":"28 Which of the following statements regarding the thor- acic sympathetic chain is incorrect? \u0001 a. It crosses the neck of the 1st rib. \u0001 b. It exits the thorax by passing beneath the lateral arcuate ligament. \u0001 c. It gives off the splanchnic nerves. \u0001 d. It gives post-ganglionic fibres to the thoracic viscera. \u0001 e. The first thoracic ganglion may be involved in the formation of the stellate ganglion.","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Thoracic Wall and Ribs","topicOriginal":"Thoracic Wall and Ribs","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Thoracic Wall and Ribs","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-c43727dce9e2","stem":"29 Which statement correctly describes the left vagus nerve? \u0001 a. It is the most anterior structure in the carotid sheath. \u0001 b. It gives a recurrent laryngeal branch which hooks around the left subclavian artery. \u0001 c. It passes posterior to the aortic arch. \u0001 d. It passes anterior to the root of the left lung. \u0001 e. Its fibres pass to the oesophagus to form the oesophageal plexus. 59 THORAX: QUESTIONS","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Thoracic Wall and Ribs","topicOriginal":"Thoracic Wall and Ribs","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Thoracic Wall and Ribs","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-dbb25c622998","stem":"30 Which of the following statements best describes the diaphragm? \u0001 a. It is attached to the xiphoid. \u0001 b. It is attached to the upper three lumbar vertebrae on the left. \u0001 c. The oesophagus passes through it at the level of T8. \u0001 d. The left phrenic nerve pierces the diaphragm with the inferior vena cava. \u0001 e. A congenital Bochdalek hernia occurs anteriorly. THORAX: ANSWERS 60 ANSWERS","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Thoracic Wall and Ribs","topicOriginal":"Thoracic Wall and Ribs","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Thoracic Wall and Ribs","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-413d6e6e9fbc","stem":"1 Which statement best describes the planes of the abdomen? \u0001 a. The transpyloric plane lies halfway between the xiphoid and the symphysis pubis. \u0001 b. The transpyloric plane passes through the hilar of the kidneys. \u0001 c. The subcostal plane is at the level of the body of L2. \u0001 d. The iliac crests lie at the level of L5. \u0001 e. The umbilicus usually lies at the level of the L4/L5 disc.","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Thoracic Wall and Ribs","topicOriginal":"Thoracic Wall and Ribs","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Thoracic Wall and Ribs","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-029c01276df2","stem":"2 Which statement about the regions of the abdomen is correct? \u0001 a. The epigastrium lies medial to the mid-clavicular line and above the transpyloric plane. \u0001 b. The suprapubic region lies between the mid- clavicular lines, the transpyloric plane and the intertubercular line. \u0001 c. The iliac fossa lies lateral to the mid-clavicular line and above the intertubercular line. \u0001 d. The hypochondrium lies below the transpyloric plane and medial to the mid-clavicular line. \u0001 e. The mid-clavicular line crosses the midpoint of the inguinal ligament. 75 76 ABDOMEN: QUESTIONS","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-45503139fe0e","stem":"3 Which statement correctly describes the abdominal wall? \u0001 a. The superficial fascia of the abdominal wall con- tains Camper’s fascia. \u0001 b. The deep fascia of the abdominal wall is known as Scarpa’s fascia. \u0001 c. Scarpa’s fascia adheres to the superficial fascia of the thigh. \u0001 d. The umbilicus receives sensory fibres from T8. \u0001 e. The groin is innervated by T12.","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Thoracic Wall and Ribs","topicOriginal":"Thoracic Wall and Ribs","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Thoracic Wall and Ribs","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-6f51c44283f4","stem":"4 Which statement about the rectus abdominis is correct? \u0001 a. It has the transversalis fascia posteriorly through- out its length. \u0001 b. It has three tendinous intersections which are vis- ible posteriorly. \u0001 c. It has the aponeuroses of the three oblique abdom- inal muscles anterior to it below the arcuate line. \u0001 d. The lower free border of the anterior rectus sheath is called the arcuate line. \u0001 e. The linea alba is a highly vascular structure.","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-75bf86777199","stem":"5 Which statement about the oblique abdominal muscles is incorrect? \u0001 a. The fibres of external oblique pass antero-inferiorly. \u0001 b. The lower fibres of internal oblique form the inguinal ligament. \u0001 c. The external oblique arises from the lower eight ribs. \u0001 d. Internal oblique arises from the lumbar fascia. \u0001 e. The internal oblique has a free lower border. 77 ABDOMEN: QUESTIONS","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Thoracic Wall and Ribs","topicOriginal":"Thoracic Wall and Ribs","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Thoracic Wall and Ribs","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-cb4d7b37a64e","stem":"6 Which statement best completes this sentence? The inguinal canal: \u0001 a. Has a deep ring, which is a defect in the transver- sus abdominis muscle. \u0001 b. Is bounded posteriorly by the inguinal ligament. \u0001 c. Has the internal oblique as part of its posterior wall throughout. \u0001 d. Has the conjoint tendon superiorly. \u0001 e. Transmits the ilioinguinal nerve, which enters the canal through the deep ring.","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-113cf2c10e08","stem":"7 Which of the following statements about the peritoneum is correct? \u0001 a. The median umbilical fold contains the obliterated remnant of the umbilical artery. \u0001 b. The greater omentum consists of four layers of peritoneum. \u0001 c. The gastrosplenic ligament contains the splenic vessels. \u0001 d. The lienorenal ligament contains the short gastric vessels. \u0001 e. The lesser omentum connects the liver to the transverse colon.","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Abdomen and GI Anatomy · Suggested","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":"Abdomen and GI Anatomy","options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Abdomen and GI Anatomy · Suggested","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-0057a89b21b2","stem":"8 Which statement about the lesser sac is not correct? \u0001 a. It is connected to the greater sac via the epiploic foramen. \u0001 b. The stomach is related anteriorly. \u0001 c. The pancreas is a posterior relation. \u0001 d. The greater omentum is an anterior relation. \u0001 e. The right border is formed by the lienorenal and gastrosplenic ligaments. 78 ABDOMEN: QUESTIONS","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Abdomen and GI Anatomy · Suggested","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":"Abdomen and GI Anatomy","options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Abdomen and GI Anatomy · Suggested","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-27fc93829431","stem":"9 Which statement about the borders of the epiploic foramen is correct? \u0001 a. The second part of the duodenum forms the infer- ior border. \u0001 b. The quadrate process of the liver forms the super- ior border. \u0001 c. The hepatic vein forms the posterior border. \u0001 d. The free edge of the greater omentum forms the anterior border. \u0001 e. The common bile duct is contained within the anterior border.","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Abdomen and GI Anatomy · Suggested","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":"Abdomen and GI Anatomy","options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Abdomen and GI Anatomy · Suggested","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-2b401745a928","stem":"10 Which of the following statements regarding peritoneal compartments is correct? \u0001 a. The infracolic compartment lies below the lesser omentum. \u0001 b. The right and left subphrenic spaces are separated by the coronary ligament. \u0001 c. The right subhepatic space lies between the right lobe of the liver and the right kidney. \u0001 d. The left subhepatic space is also known as the hepatorenal pouch. \u0001 e. The right paracolic gutter lies medial to the colon.","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Cardiovascular Anatomy","topicOriginal":"Cardiovascular Anatomy","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Cardiovascular Anatomy","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-fe0cd29f881e","stem":"11 Which of these statements about the coeliac trunk is not correct? \u0001 a. It supplies the foregut and its derivatives. \u0001 b. It leaves the aorta at the level of L1. \u0001 c. It gives a left gastric branch that supplies the oesophagus. \u0001 d. It gives a splenic branch. \u0001 e. It gives rise to the gastroduodenal artery via its hepatic branch. 79 ABDOMEN: QUESTIONS","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Mediastinum","topicOriginal":"Mediastinum","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Mediastinum","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-2221b5b67fee","stem":"12 Which statement best describes the blood supply of the stomach? \u0001 a. It is derived entirely from the superior mesenteric artery. \u0001 b. The gastroepiploic arteries supply the lesser curvature. \u0001 c. The right gastric artery is a direct branch of the coeliac axis. \u0001 d. The left gastroepiploic artery arises directly from the coeliac trunk. \u0001 e. The short gastric arteries arise from the splenic artery.","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Thoracic Wall and Ribs","topicOriginal":"Thoracic Wall and Ribs","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Thoracic Wall and Ribs","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-9ce1efca55fd","stem":"13 Which statement best describes the venous drainage of the alimentary tract? \u0001 a. All blood drains into the portal system. \u0001 b. The portal vein is formed from the union of the inferior mesenteric and splenic veins. \u0001 c. The superior mesenteric vein crosses the uncinate process of the pancreas. \u0001 d. The inferior mesenteric vein passes behind the left renal vein. \u0001 e. The prepyloric vein is variable in position.","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Thoracic Wall and Ribs","topicOriginal":"Thoracic Wall and Ribs","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Thoracic Wall and Ribs","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-be0d43c1dabb","stem":"14 Which statement best completes this sentence? The superior mesenteric artery: \u0001 a. Supplies the gut from the pylorus to the terminal ileum. \u0001 b. Arises from the aorta at the level of L1. \u0001 c. Runs in front of the body of the pancreas. \u0001 d. Crosses the second part of the duodenum. \u0001 e. Supplies the appendix via its right colic branch. 80 ABDOMEN: QUESTIONS","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Abdomen and GI Anatomy · Suggested","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":"Abdomen and GI Anatomy","options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Abdomen and GI Anatomy · Suggested","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-a63d87da5e2f","stem":"15 Which statement best describes the inferior mesenteric artery? \u0001 a. It arises from the aorta at the level of the transpy- loric plane. \u0001 b. It supplies the mucus membrane of the gut as far as the mid-rectum. \u0001 c. It gives off a left colic branch. \u0001 d. It crosses the pelvic brim at the point of bifurca- tion of the right common iliac vessels. \u0001 e. It anastomoses with the superior mesenteric artery via its sigmoid branch.","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Thoracic Wall and Ribs","topicOriginal":"Thoracic Wall and Ribs","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Thoracic Wall and Ribs","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-06b9963f5d57","stem":"16 Which statement best describes the lymphatics of the gastrointestinal tract? \u0001 a. They generally follow routes which are distinct from those taken by the venous drainage of the bowel. \u0001 b. Peyer’s patches are found on the mesenteric sur- face of the large bowel. \u0001 c. Lymphoid follicles become less numerous in the distal part of the gut. \u0001 d. Preaortic nodes lie at the origins of major blood vessels. \u0001 e. Lymph from the alimentary tract eventually passes into the portal system of veins.","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Thoracic Wall and Ribs","topicOriginal":"Thoracic Wall and Ribs","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Thoracic Wall and Ribs","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-c358ba66b916","stem":"17 Which statement best describes the stomach? \u0001 a. Lymph from the superior 2/3 of the stomach drains into the suprapancreatic nodes. \u0001 b. All lymph from the stomach drains through the coeliac nodes. \u0001 c. The gastric branches of the vagi are given of in the greater curve. \u0001 d. The lower oesophageal sphinchter is supplied by the nerves of Latarjet. \u0001 e. The antral part of the stomach secretes an acid solution. 81 ABDOMEN: QUESTIONS","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Thoracic Wall and Ribs","topicOriginal":"Thoracic Wall and Ribs","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Thoracic Wall and Ribs","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-528e03a67eb0","stem":"18 Which of the following statements about the duodenum is incorrect? \u0001 a. The second part overlies the right kidney. \u0001 b. The transverse mesocolon attaches over the second part. \u0001 c. The ampulla of Vater lies in the third part. \u0001 d. The inferior vena cava and aorta lie directly behind the third part. \u0001 e. The gall bladder overlies the first part.","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Abdomen and GI Anatomy · Suggested","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":"Abdomen and GI Anatomy","options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Abdomen and GI Anatomy · Suggested","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-fca80f045cdb","stem":"19 Which statement regarding the jejunum and ileum is incorrect? \u0001 a. The ileum has thicker walls than the jejunum. \u0001 b. The proximal small intestine is of greater diam- eter than the distal. \u0001 c. The mesentery of the small intestine is thicker distally. \u0001 d. The jejunum lies mainly in the umbilical region. \u0001 e. The mesenteric vessels form more numerous arcades in the ileum.","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Abdomen and GI Anatomy · Suggested","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":"Abdomen and GI Anatomy","options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Abdomen and GI Anatomy · Suggested","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-8a81a9a4043e","stem":"20 Which of these statements regarding Meckel’s diverticu- lum is correct? \u0001 a. It is found in about 4% of the population. \u0001 b. Is always found on the antimesenteric border of the ileum. \u0001 c. Is usually about 2 cm in length. \u0001 d. Is usually located about 20 cm from the ileocaecal junction. \u0001 e. Is usually attached to the umbilicus. 82 ABDOMEN: QUESTIONS","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Abdomen and GI Anatomy · Suggested","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":"Abdomen and GI Anatomy","options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Abdomen and GI Anatomy · Suggested","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-c7bbb71deced","stem":"21 Which statement about the appendix is incorrect? \u0001 a. The taenia coli converge on the base of the appendix. \u0001 b. The mesoappendix contains the appendicular vessels in its free edge. \u0001 c. The ileocaecal fold connects the appendix to the ileum. \u0001 d. The appendix always lies behind the ileum. \u0001 e. The appendix opens into the posteromedial part of the caecum.","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Abdomen and GI Anatomy · Suggested","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":"Abdomen and GI Anatomy","options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Abdomen and GI Anatomy · Suggested","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-fad838afca45","stem":"22 Which of the following statements best describes the colon? \u0001 a. The majority is derived from the primitive hindgut. \u0001 b. It is usually invested with peritoneum on all sides. \u0001 c. The hepatic flexure is related directly to the liver and right kidney. \u0001 d. The ascending and descending colon are always retroperitoneal. \u0001 e. The appendices epiploicae are composed of lymph- oid tissue.","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Thoracic Wall and Ribs","topicOriginal":"Thoracic Wall and Ribs","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Thoracic Wall and Ribs","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-6c22898b55a3","stem":"23 Which statement most correctly describes the large bowel? \u0001 a. The taenia coli are longer than the colon. \u0001 b. The descending colon is the longest part. \u0001 c. The sigmoid colon has a mesentery. \u0001 d. The parasympathetic supply is entirely derived from the vagus nerve. \u0001 e. The marginal artery lies within the bowel wall. 83 ABDOMEN: QUESTIONS","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Thoracic Wall and Ribs","topicOriginal":"Thoracic Wall and Ribs","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Thoracic Wall and Ribs","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-2e65c3ce2940","stem":"24 Which statement about the liver is correct? \u0001 a. The gall bladder lies directly anterior to the fis- sure for the ligamentum venosum. \u0001 b. The ligamentum teres contains the falciform ligament. \u0001 c. The caudate lobe lies anterior to the quadrate lobe. \u0001 d. The liver is closely related to the abdominal oesophagus. \u0001 e. The functional lobes of the liver are demarcated by a line drawn between the attachment of the falciform ligament and the fissure for the liga- mentum venosum.","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Mediastinum","topicOriginal":"Mediastinum","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Mediastinum","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-bcff9e2f5f79","stem":"25 Which statement best completes this sentence? The porta hepatis contains: \u0001 a. The hepatic artery on the left side. \u0001 b. The common hepatic duct anteriorly. \u0001 c. The portal vein anteriorly. \u0001 d. Parasympathetic fibres from the vagus. \u0001 e. Sympathetic fibres from the hypogastric plexus.","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Mediastinum","topicOriginal":"Mediastinum","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Mediastinum","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-e89c5695164a","stem":"26 When considering porto-systemic anastomosis, which of the following statements is correct? \u0001 a. The portal vein is formed by the union of the superior and inferior mesenteric veins. \u0001 b. There is an anastomosis between the middle and inferior rectal veins. \u0001 c. There is an anastomosis between the azygos sys- tem and right gastric veins. \u0001 d. There is a direct anastomosis between the para- umbilical and common iliac veins. \u0001 e. There is an anastomosis between the colic and retroperitoneal veins. 84 ABDOMEN: QUESTIONS","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Abdomen and GI Anatomy · Suggested","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":"Abdomen and GI Anatomy","options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Abdomen and GI Anatomy · Suggested","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-15b8dcaa2899","stem":"27 Which statement concerning the peritoneal reflections on the liver is not correct? \u0001 a. The bare area lies between the left and right tri- angular ligaments. \u0001 b. The coronary ligament is continuous with the fal- ciform ligament. \u0001 c. The inferior vena cava forms a border of the bare area. \u0001 d. The lesser omentum is reflected around the struc- tures of the porta hepatis. \u0001 e. The bare area is in contact with the right supra- renal gland.","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Cardiovascular Anatomy","topicOriginal":"Cardiovascular Anatomy","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Cardiovascular Anatomy","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-5340ae174d69","stem":"28 Which of the following statements about the biliary sys- tem is correct? \u0001 a. The pancreatic duct drains into the hepatic duct. \u0001 b. The common hepatic duct is formed by the union of the common bile and cystic ducts. \u0001 c. Callot’s triangle is formed by the hepatic duct, cystic duct and the cystic artery. \u0001 d. The common bile duct runs behind the first part of the duodenum. \u0001 e. The common bile duct runs between the head of the pancreas and the third part of the duodenum.","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Abdomen and GI Anatomy · Suggested","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":"Abdomen and GI Anatomy","options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Abdomen and GI Anatomy · Suggested","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-9ddb7d3eeb8e","stem":"29 Which statement concerning the relations of the kid- neys is correct? \u0001 a. The diaphragm is directly related to the superior pole of both kidneys. \u0001 b. The left kidney is related to the duodenum. \u0001 c. The kidneys are posteriorly related to quadratus lumborum. \u0001 d. The pancreas is related to the left kidney above the stomach. \u0001 e. The right kidney is related to the descending colon. 85 ABDOMEN: QUESTIONS","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Diaphragm","topicOriginal":"Diaphragm","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Diaphragm","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-b49a2729abda","stem":"30 Which statement about the fascia of the kidney is true? \u0001 a. The suprarenals lie in the same fascial compart- ment as the kidneys. \u0001 b. The renal fascia is continuous with fascia over the inferior vena cava and aorta. \u0001 c. The perinephric fat lies outside the renal fascia. \u0001 d. The lienorenal ligament is attached to the right kidney. \u0001 e. The perinephric fat lies deep to the true capsule of the kidney.","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-f80bb646e9b6","stem":"31 Which of these statements about the pancreas is not correct? \u0001 a. The neck lies in the subcostal plane. \u0001 b. It is supplied by the splenic artery. \u0001 c. The uncinate process drains via the accessory pancreatic duct. \u0001 d. It is related to the second part of the duodenum. \u0001 e. Develops from two buds which arise from the primitive foregut.","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Thoracic Wall and Ribs","topicOriginal":"Thoracic Wall and Ribs","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Thoracic Wall and Ribs","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-1afbab6ce436","stem":"32 Which of the following does not correctly describe the spleen? \u0001 a. Has a notched superior border. \u0001 b. Lies under the 9th to 11th ribs. \u0001 c. Is related directly to the left kidney and the stomach. \u0001 d. Is completely invested in peritoneum. \u0001 e. The tail of the pancreas is related to the hilum.","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Thoracic Wall and Ribs","topicOriginal":"Thoracic Wall and Ribs","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Thoracic Wall and Ribs","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-0683e35509d4","stem":"33 Which statement best describes psoas major? \u0001 a. The femoral nerve emerges medial to it. \u0001 b. The obturator nerve pierces the muscle. \u0001 c. The genitofemoral nerve emerges lateral to it. \u0001 d. It is attached distally to the intertrochanteric line. \u0001 e. The medial arcuate ligament is a condensation of its fascia. 86 ABDOMEN: QUESTIONS","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Thoracic Wall and Ribs","topicOriginal":"Thoracic Wall and Ribs","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Thoracic Wall and Ribs","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-b9887327b38e","stem":"34 Which statement correctly completes the following sen- tence? The abdominal aorta: \u0001 a. Has four lumbar branches. \u0001 b. Is crossed by the body of the pancreas between the origins of the superior mesenteric artery and inferior mesenteric artery. \u0001 c. Has the subcostal artery as its first branch. \u0001 d. Ends at the level of the L5/S1 disc. \u0001 e. Gives three direct branches to the suprarenals.","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Thoracic Wall and Ribs","topicOriginal":"Thoracic Wall and Ribs","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Thoracic Wall and Ribs","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-45a145c0ee85","stem":"35 Which statement correctly describes the inferior vena cava? \u0001 a. It commences at the level of L3. \u0001 b. It has gastrointestinal tributaries which corre- spond to the equivalent branches of the aorta. \u0001 c. The second lumbar vein is a tributary. \u0001 d. The subcostal vein is a tributary. \u0001 e. The hepatic veins are tributaries.","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Thoracic Wall and Ribs","topicOriginal":"Thoracic Wall and Ribs","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Thoracic Wall and Ribs","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-766902713476","stem":"36 Which statement about the lumbar plexus is incorrect? \u0001 a. It is formed by the anterior primary rami of L1–L4. \u0001 b. The ilioinguinal and iliohypogastric nerves are both derived from L3. \u0001 c. The femoral and obturator nerves are both derived from the anterior primary rami of L2–L4. \u0001 d. A branch of the genitofemoral nerve traverses the inguinal canal. \u0001 e. It is connected to the sacral plexus by the lum- bosacral trunk. 87 ABDOMEN: QUESTIONS","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Lower Limb · Suggested","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":"Lower Limb","options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Lower Limb · Suggested","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-4a32cf2fb06c","stem":"37 Which statement concerning the abdominal autonomic supply is incorrect? \u0001 a. The coeliac ganglia receive fibres from the greater and lesser splanchnic nerves. \u0001 b. The abdominal organs all derive their sympa- thetic supply from the coeliac ganglia. \u0001 c. The greater splanchnic nerve contains fibres which innervate the adrenal cortex. \u0001 d. The sympathetic system contains motor fibres to the sphincters. \u0001 e. The pelvic splanchnic nerves contain parasympa- thetic fibres.","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Pelvis and Perineum · Suggested","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":"Pelvis and Perineum","options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Pelvis and Perineum · Suggested","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-62d16c803552","stem":"38 Which statement best describes the ureter? \u0001 a. Is narrowest as it crosses the transverse process of L4. \u0001 b. Is crossed anteriorly by the gonadal vessels. \u0001 c. Is crossed anteriorly by the root of the mesentery on the left. \u0001 d. It courses superior to the uterine vessels. \u0001 e. Has a blood supply from the external iliac artery.","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Thoracic Wall and Ribs","topicOriginal":"Thoracic Wall and Ribs","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Thoracic Wall and Ribs","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-8f35513ba3b0","stem":"39 Which of the following structures does the ureter cross on an abdominal radiograph? \u0001 a. The bodies of the lumbar vertebrae. \u0001 b. Ischial tuberosity. \u0001 c. Sacroiliac joint. \u0001 d. Pubic tubercle. \u0001 e. Tragal pointer. ABDOMEN: ANSWERS 88 ANSWERS","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Pelvis and Perineum · Suggested","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":"Pelvis and Perineum","options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Pelvis and Perineum · Suggested","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-7cd4a7a63cae","stem":"1 Which of the following statements best describes the bony pelvis? \u0001 a. The true pelvis lies between the iliac crests. \u0001 b. The acetabulum is formed by contributions from all parts of the inominate bone. \u0001 c. The male pelvic inlet has a more oval shape than in the female. \u0001 d. The angle between the inferior pubic rami is wider in the male. \u0001 e. The pelvic outlet is between the symphysis pubis and the sacral tuberosity.","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Thoracic Wall and Ribs","topicOriginal":"Thoracic Wall and Ribs","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Thoracic Wall and Ribs","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-ff607ef461a1","stem":"2 Which of the following statements about the ligaments of the pelvis is incorrect? \u0001 a. The inguinal ligament passes from the anterior superior iliac spine to the pubic tubercle. \u0001 b. The sacrospinous ligament is a border of the lesser sciatic foramen. \u0001 c. The sacrotuberous ligament is a border of the lesser sciatic foramen. \u0001 d. The lacunar ligament forms the lateral border of the femoral canal. \u0001 e. The sacrospinous ligament provides an attachment for coccygeus. 109 110 PELVIS: QUESTIONS","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Lower Limb · Suggested","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":"Lower Limb","options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Lower Limb · Suggested","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-7a27cc5c81b2","stem":"3 Which of the following associations of joints and types is correct? \u0001 a. Symphysis pubis Primary cartilaginous \u0001 b. Sacroiliac joint Synovial \u0001 c. Lumbosacral joint Primary cartilaginous \u0001 d. Acetabulofemoral Fibrous \u0001 e. Sacrococcygeal Fibrous","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-9c4a3927b29d","stem":"4 Which of the following structures does not pass through the greater sciatic foramen? \u0001 a. Piriformis. \u0001 b. Sciatic nerve. \u0001 c. Superior gluteal nerve. \u0001 d. Inferior gluteal nerve. \u0001 e. Gemellus superior.","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Lower Limb · Suggested","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":"Lower Limb","options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Lower Limb · Suggested","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-12f0cd65f101","stem":"5 Which of the following structures passes through the lesser sciatic foramen? \u0001 a. The posterior cutaneous nerve of the thigh. \u0001 b. The pudendal nerve. \u0001 c. Obturator externus. \u0001 d. Gemellus superior. \u0001 e. Gemellus inferior.","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Lower Limb · Suggested","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":"Lower Limb","options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Lower Limb · Suggested","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-a9bd51fceabe","stem":"6 Which statement most correctly completes the following sentence? Levator ani: \u0001 a. Attaches to the fascia over obturator internus. \u0001 b. Has fibres which assist continence by pulling the rectum posteriorly. \u0001 c. Lies inferior to the ischiorectal fossa. \u0001 d. Is supplied by anterior primary rami of S1 and S2. \u0001 e. Contracts during defaecation. 111 PELVIS: QUESTIONS","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Pelvis and Perineum · Suggested","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":"Pelvis and Perineum","options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Pelvis and Perineum · Suggested","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-d9a2dac0704b","stem":"7 Which statement about the perineum is the correct one? \u0001 a. It is divided by a line drawn between the ischial spines. \u0001 b. The perineal body lies between the anterior and posterior parts of the perineum. \u0001 c. The perineal membrane lies in the posterior part of the perineum. \u0001 d. Bartholin’s glands lie deep to the perineal membrane. \u0001 e. The posterior part of the perineum is bounded lat- erally by the sacrospinous ligaments.","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Pelvis and Perineum · Suggested","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":"Pelvis and Perineum","options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Pelvis and Perineum · Suggested","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-882169d0f07c","stem":"8 Which statement about the rectum and its relations is incorrect? \u0001 a. There are two rectal folds on the left. \u0001 b. The anorectal junction is related to the puborecta- lis muscle. \u0001 c. The upper 1/3 is entirely invested in peritoneum. \u0001 d. The pararectal lymph nodes lie in the mesorectum. \u0001 e. Denonvillier’s fascia lies below the rectovesical pouch.","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Abdomen and GI Anatomy · Suggested","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":"Abdomen and GI Anatomy","options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Abdomen and GI Anatomy · Suggested","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-08bdf68c8187","stem":"9 Which statement best describes the anus and its sphincters? \u0001 a. The anus contains longitudinal and circular muscle. \u0001 b. The external sphincter is composed of involuntary muscle. \u0001 c. The external sphincter is continuous with the muscle of the rectum. \u0001 d. The anorectal ring is formed by puborectalis. \u0001 e. The anal cushions are found in the left lateral, right posterior and right anterior positions. 112 PELVIS: QUESTIONS","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Thoracic Wall and Ribs","topicOriginal":"Thoracic Wall and Ribs","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Thoracic Wall and Ribs","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-e38fb3399270","stem":"10 Which statement best describes the bladder? \u0001 a. The interureteric bar joins the two ureteric orifices. \u0001 b. The venous drainage follows the course of the arterial supply. \u0001 c. It has a blood supply principally derived from the external iliac artery. \u0001 d. It is lined by specialised non-keratinising squa- mous epithelium. \u0001 e. The sensation of bladder fullness is conveyed by sympathetic fibres.","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Thoracic Wall and Ribs","topicOriginal":"Thoracic Wall and Ribs","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Thoracic Wall and Ribs","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-1d95c0945b9c","stem":"11 Which statement best describes the pelvic viscera? \u0001 a. The vas deferens is formed by the union of the ejaculatory ducts and the seminal vesicles. \u0001 b. The vas deferens runs through the inguinal canal. \u0001 c. The vas deferens passes anterior to the urethra to enter the bladder. \u0001 d. The ejaculatory ducts enter the spongy urethra. \u0001 e. The bulbourethral glands enter the prostatic urethra.","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Thoracic Wall and Ribs","topicOriginal":"Thoracic Wall and Ribs","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Thoracic Wall and Ribs","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-2d9e3e348914","stem":"12 Which statement about the prostate is incorrect? \u0001 a. The urethal crest is found on the posterior wall of the urethra. \u0001 b. It lies between the bladder and the external ureth- ral sphincter. \u0001 c. The urethra runs through the posterior part of the gland. \u0001 d. It may have a true and a false capsule. \u0001 e. It is supplied by the inferior vesical arteries. 113 PELVIS: QUESTIONS","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Pelvis and Perineum · Suggested","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":"Pelvis and Perineum","options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Pelvis and Perineum · Suggested","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-abd04df6b20a","stem":"13 Which statement regarding the male perineum is incorrect? \u0001 a. The root of the penis is attached to the perineal membrane. \u0001 b. The membranous urethra lies superficial to the perineal membrane. \u0001 c. Cowper’s glands lie deep to the perineal membrane. \u0001 d. Colles’ fascia is attached to the perineal membrane. \u0001 e. The sphincter urethrae contains voluntary muscle.","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Pelvis and Perineum · Suggested","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":"Pelvis and Perineum","options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Pelvis and Perineum · Suggested","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-088f2ac7998d","stem":"14 Which statement best describes the spermatic cord? \u0001 a. The internal spermatic fascia is derived from the transversus abdominis muscle. \u0001 b. The cremasteric fascia contains muscle fibres derived from external oblique. \u0001 c. It contains the genital branch of the genitofemo- ral nerve. \u0001 d. It contains the ilioinguinal nerve. \u0001 e. It contains the testicular vein.","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Thoracic Wall and Ribs","topicOriginal":"Thoracic Wall and Ribs","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Thoracic Wall and Ribs","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-63643210f487","stem":"15 Which statement best describes the testis and epididymis? \u0001 a. The vas deferens arises from the upper pole of the epididymis. \u0001 b. The appendix testis is attached to the lower pole of the testis. \u0001 c. The tunica vaginalis lies deep to the tunica albuginea. \u0001 d. The testis is divided into lobules by septa. \u0001 e. The dartos muscle lies deep to the cremasteric fascia. 114 PELVIS: QUESTIONS","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Thoracic Wall and Ribs","topicOriginal":"Thoracic Wall and Ribs","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Thoracic Wall and Ribs","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-63049409dee2","stem":"16 Which statement concerning the penis is incorrect? \u0001 a. The corpora cavernosa are ensheathed by the tunica albuginea. \u0001 b. The penile urethra runs within the corpus spongiosum. \u0001 c. There are two deep penile arteries. \u0001 d. Erection is under sympathetic control. \u0001 e. Bulbospongiosus is important in micturition.","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-5b50e0fa04a8","stem":"17 Which statement about the uterus and fallopian tubes is correct? \u0001 a. The fundus lies posterior to the entrance of the fallopian tubes into the uterus. \u0001 b. The cornu is the point of junction of the body of the uterus and the vagina. \u0001 c. The ampulla of the fallopian tubes bare fimbrae. \u0001 d. The fallopian tubes contain smooth muscle. \u0001 e. The upper part drains lymph to the external iliac nodes.","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Pelvis and Perineum · Suggested","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":"Pelvis and Perineum","options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Pelvis and Perineum · Suggested","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-2fef55700384","stem":"18 Which of the following statements about the uterus is incorrect? \u0001 a. The uterus is retroverted in 1/5 of nulliparous females. \u0001 b. The uterine artery courses down the lateral wall of the uterus. \u0001 c. The cervix is attached to the bladder. \u0001 d. Develops from the paramesonephric ducts. \u0001 e. The cardinal ligament is traversed by the ureter.","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Pelvis and Perineum · Suggested","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":"Pelvis and Perineum","options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Pelvis and Perineum · Suggested","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-9a48120dd9b0","stem":"19 Which statement concerning the ovary is the correct one? \u0001 a. The ovarian ligament contains the ovarian vessels. \u0001 b. The right ovarian vein drains into the right renal vein. \u0001 c. The suspensory ligament of the ovary is attached to the sidewall of the pelvis. \u0001 d. Lymphatics drain to the internal iliac nodes. \u0001 e. It is attached to the anterior aspect of the broad ligament. 115 PELVIS: QUESTIONS","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Pelvis and Perineum · Suggested","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":"Pelvis and Perineum","options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Pelvis and Perineum · Suggested","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-6f1f33a65039","stem":"20 Which structure is not contained within, or attached to, the broad ligament? \u0001 a. The suspensory ligament of the ovary. \u0001 b. The round ligament. \u0001 c. The uterine artery. \u0001 d. The fallopian tube. \u0001 e. The ovarian ligament.","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-9ba575ff3918","stem":"21 Which statement best describes the cervix and vagina? \u0001 a. The anterior part of the cervix is covered with peritoneum. \u0001 b. The vagina is covered with peritoneum anteriorly. \u0001 c. The ureter is a close relation of the cervix. \u0001 d. The entire vagina is supplied with sensory fibres by the pudendal nerve. \u0001 e. The majority of the lymph from the vagina drains to the superficial inguinal nodes.","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Thoracic Wall and Ribs","topicOriginal":"Thoracic Wall and Ribs","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Thoracic Wall and Ribs","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-2e7599c2dc3c","stem":"22 Which statement about the pelvic vasculature is correct? \u0001 a. The common iliac artery divides over the sacro- iliac joint. \u0001 b. The external iliac artery divides into anterior and posterior branches. \u0001 c. The ovarian artery is a branch of the internal iliac artery. \u0001 d. The inferior epigastric artery is a branch of the internal iliac artery. \u0001 e. The obturator artery is a branch of the external iliac artery. 116 PELVIS: QUESTIONS","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Pelvis and Perineum · Suggested","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":"Pelvis and Perineum","options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Pelvis and Perineum · Suggested","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-0177ec2f9327","stem":"23 Which statement regarding the nerve supply of mictu- rition is true? \u0001 a. Contraction of the detrusor muscle is initiated by fibres from the inferior hypogastric plexus. \u0001 b. The sphincter urethrae is innervated by the para- sympathetic system. \u0001 c. The internal urethral sphincter is parasympathet- ically innervated. \u0001 d. Pubovaginalis helps to maintain continence in the female. \u0001 e. Following a lumbar cord transection, bladder voiding is impossible.","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-e8ff9956f1fa","stem":"24 Which statement does not correctly complete this sen- tence? The pudendal nerve: \u0001 a. Enters the pelvis through the lesser sciatic foramen. \u0001 b. Runs in a canal formed by the obturator internus fascia. \u0001 c. Has a dorsal branch which is sensory to the penis. \u0001 d. Has a perineal branch which is sensory to the clitoris. \u0001 e. Gives motor fibres to the muscles of the urogeni- tal diaphragm. PELVIS: ANSWERS 117 ANSWERS","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Diaphragm","topicOriginal":"Diaphragm","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Diaphragm","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-b99b375067f7","stem":"1 Which statement about the neurocranium is correct? \u0001 a. The bregma is the adult remnant of the posterior fontanelle. \u0001 b. The metopic suture usually disappears in the first year of life. \u0001 c. The anterior fontanelle is not palpable past 1 year of age. \u0001 d. Six bones make up the adult neurocranium. \u0001 e. The pterion represents the thinnest part of the lat- eral skull.","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Head and Neck · Suggested","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":"Head and Neck","options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Head and Neck · Suggested","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-5f464694ffb6","stem":"2 Which statement regarding the foramina of the skull is correct? \u0001 a. The inferior ophthalmic vein passes through the inferior orbital fissure. \u0001 b. The foramen spinosum connects the middle cra- nial fossa with the infratemporal fossa. \u0001 c. The foramen ovale transmits the maxillary div- ision of the trigeminal nerve. \u0001 d. The optic nerve enters the skull in the anterior cranial fossa. \u0001 e. The hypoglossal nerve exits the skull via the jugu- lar foramen. 129 130 HEAD AND NECK: QUESTIONS","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Head and Neck","topicOriginal":"Head and Neck","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Head and Neck","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-702e138bcab1","stem":"3 Which statement best describes the cranial fossae? \u0001 a. The middle cranial fossa contains the cribriform plate. \u0001 b. The anterior cranial fossa contains the pituitary gland. \u0001 c. The middle cranial fossa is floored by the sphenoid and temporal bones. \u0001 d. The internal acoustic meatus lies in the middle cranial fossa. \u0001 e. The occipital lobe lies in the posterior cranial fossa.","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Thoracic Wall and Ribs","topicOriginal":"Thoracic Wall and Ribs","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Thoracic Wall and Ribs","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-d2bd8852f36f","stem":"4 Which statement concerning the development of the face is incorrect? \u0001 a. Frontonasal process forms the nose. \u0001 b. Frontonasal process forms the premaxilla. \u0001 c. Maxillary processes fuse in the midline. \u0001 d. Maxillary processes forms the entire palate. \u0001 e. Mandibular processes fuse in the midline.","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Head and Neck · Suggested","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":"Head and Neck","options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Head and Neck · Suggested","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-f539db33eeee","stem":"5 Which statement best describes the branchial arches and their derivatives? \u0001 a. Five branchial arches form in human embryos. \u0001 b. The ossicles of the middle ear are all skeletal deriva- tives of the first arch. \u0001 c. The muscles of facial expression are derived from the second arch. \u0001 d. The nerve of the third arch is the vagus. \u0001 e. The artery of the first arch forms part of the internal carotid artery. 131 HEAD AND NECK: QUESTIONS","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Thoracic Wall and Ribs","topicOriginal":"Thoracic Wall and Ribs","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Thoracic Wall and Ribs","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-f8c0841b587a","stem":"6 Which of the following statements about the eye are incorrect? \u0001 a. Aqueous humour is drained via the canal of Schlemm. \u0001 b. Greatest visual acuity is found at the macula densa. \u0001 c. The optic nerve is invested by all three meningeal layers. \u0001 d. The central artery of the retina is an end artery. \u0001 e. Parasympathetic fibres from the oculomotor nerve innervate the lacrimal gland.","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Neuroanatomy · Suggested","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":"Neuroanatomy","options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Neuroanatomy · Suggested","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-608347b0598b","stem":"7 Which statement best describes the muscles of the eye? \u0001 a. The superior and inferior obliques arise from the tendinous ring. \u0001 b. The superior oblique is innervated by the oculo- motor nerve. \u0001 c. Sphincter pupillae is innervated by sympathetic nerves. \u0001 d. Oculomotor nerve paralysis causes a complete ptosis. \u0001 e. Closure of the eye is controlled by the facial nerve.","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Thoracic Wall and Ribs","topicOriginal":"Thoracic Wall and Ribs","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Thoracic Wall and Ribs","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-fcb3deb10515","stem":"8 Which statement best describes the middle ear? \u0001 a. The medial wall is formed by the tympanic membrane. \u0001 b. The epitympanic recess contains the incus and head of the stapes. \u0001 c. The internal jugular vein is related to its roof. \u0001 d. It communicates with the mastoid air cells inferiorly. \u0001 e. The Eustachian tube runs from the anterior wall of the middle ear to the oropharynx. 132 HEAD AND NECK: QUESTIONS","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Thoracic Wall and Ribs","topicOriginal":"Thoracic Wall and Ribs","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Thoracic Wall and Ribs","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-a216a30a26dd","stem":"9 Which statement about the nasal septum is correct? \u0001 a. Is formed by the septal cartilage posteriorly. \u0001 b. Is formed by the perpendicular plate of the eth- moid postero-inferiorly. \u0001 c. Is lined by mucoperiosteum throughout. \u0001 d. Little’s area overlies the vomer. \u0001 e. The sphenopalatine artery courses over the septum.","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Head and Neck · Suggested","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":"Head and Neck","options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Head and Neck · Suggested","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-199a31404b84","stem":"10 Consider the lateral wall of the nasal cavity, which state- ment describes it the best? \u0001 a. It is formed principally by the ethmoid bone. \u0001 b. The nasal bone forms the anterior part of the lat- eral wall. \u0001 c. The inferior concha is part of the maxilla. \u0001 d. The superior concha is part of the maxilla. \u0001 e. The superior meatus lies above the superior turbinate.","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Thoracic Wall and Ribs","topicOriginal":"Thoracic Wall and Ribs","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Thoracic Wall and Ribs","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-7f1927a6404f","stem":"11 Which statement about the maxillary sinus is incorrect? \u0001 a. Is lined by respiratory mucous membrane. \u0001 b. Forms the floor of the orbit via its roof. \u0001 c. Is drained from its most dependent part. \u0001 d. Drains into the middle meatus. \u0001 e. Is supplied by branches of the maxillary nerve.","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Head and Neck","topicOriginal":"Head and Neck","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Head and Neck","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-acbfe69b4802","stem":"12 Which statement describing the ethmoid sinuses is not correct? \u0001 a. They lie between the orbit and the nasal cavity. \u0001 b. They form six distinct cavities. \u0001 c. The anterior ethmoidal air cells drain into the middle meatus. \u0001 d. The posterior ethmoidal air cells drain into the superior meatus. \u0001 e. Are supplied by branches from both the oph- thalmic and maxillary branches of the trigeminal nerve. 133 HEAD AND NECK: QUESTIONS","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Thoracic Wall and Ribs","topicOriginal":"Thoracic Wall and Ribs","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Thoracic Wall and Ribs","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-50f265b9bcf9","stem":"13 Which statement concerning the paranasal sinuses is not correct? \u0001 a. The sphenoid sinuses are medial to the cavernous sinus. \u0001 b. The sphenoid sinuses open into the nasal cavity behind the superior concha. \u0001 c. The frontal sinuses are not present at birth. \u0001 d. The frontal sinus is related to the orbit. \u0001 e. The frontal sinus drains into the superior meatus.","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Head and Neck","topicOriginal":"Head and Neck","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Head and Neck","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-9380647a537d","stem":"14 Which of the following statements does not correctly describe the oral cavity? \u0001 a. Taste sensation for the posterior 1/3 of the tongue is supplied by the glossopharyngeal nerve. \u0001 b. The sensory innervation for the anterior 2/3 of the tongue is provided by the trigeminal nerve. \u0001 c. The four extrinsic muscles of the tongue are sup- plied by the hypoglossal nerve. \u0001 d. Lesions of the hypoglossal nerve cause ipsilateral deviation of the tongue on protrusion. \u0001 e. The teeth are innervated by divisions of the trigeminal nerve.","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Thoracic Wall and Ribs","topicOriginal":"Thoracic Wall and Ribs","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Thoracic Wall and Ribs","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-3be004c8fd29","stem":"15 Which statement best describes the anatomy of the tongue? \u0001 a. The anterior 1/3 is separated from the posterior 2/3 by the sulcus terminalis. \u0001 b. Receives its blood supply from a direct branch of the external carotid artery. \u0001 c. The posterior 1/3 drains lymph to the lower part of the deep cervical chain of lymph nodes. \u0001 d. Common sensation to the anterior 2/3 of the tongue is derived from the facial nerve. \u0001 e. The intrinsic muscles of the tongue are all inner- vated by the facial nerve. 134 HEAD AND NECK: QUESTIONS","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Thoracic Wall and Ribs","topicOriginal":"Thoracic Wall and Ribs","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Thoracic Wall and Ribs","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-e5478cf02ccc","stem":"16 Which of the following is not true of the palatine tonsil? \u0001 a. Has the palatopharyngeal arch as a direct anterior relation. \u0001 b. Is separated by its capsule from the superior con- strictor of the pharynx. \u0001 c. Derives its blood supply principally from the facial artery. \u0001 d. Has the paratonsillar vein as a lateral relation. \u0001 e. Drains its lymph to nodes along the internal jugu- lar vein.","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Head and Neck","topicOriginal":"Head and Neck","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Head and Neck","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-bb8a5e939cbe","stem":"17 Which statement incorrectly describes the muscles of mastication? \u0001 a. Dislocation of the temporomandibular joint usu- ally occurs anteriorly. \u0001 b. The medial pterygoid takes origin from the lateral pterygoid plate. \u0001 c. Of the muscles of mastication only the lateral pterygoid is involved in protracting the mandible. \u0001 d. All the muscles of mastication are innervated by the maxillary division of the trigeminal nerve. \u0001 e. All of the muscles of mastication are supplied by branches of the maxillary artery.","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Thoracic Wall and Ribs","topicOriginal":"Thoracic Wall and Ribs","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Thoracic Wall and Ribs","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-3dce63879be3","stem":"18 Which statement regarding the parotid gland is correct? \u0001 a. The parotid duct opens into the oral cavity oppos- ite the second lower molar tooth. \u0001 b. The parotid duct runs deep to masseter through- out its course. \u0001 c. Preganglionic parasympathetic fibres innervat- ing the parotid originate in the superior salivary nucleus. \u0001 d. Postganglionic parasympathetic fibres originate in the submandibular ganglion. \u0001 e. Sympathetic innervation to the parotid follows the external carotid artery. 135 HEAD AND NECK: QUESTIONS","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Head and Neck","topicOriginal":"Head and Neck","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Head and Neck","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-27d0a83f1230","stem":"19 Which of the following statements best describes the salivary glands? \u0001 a. The stylomandibular ligament separates the sub- mandibular and sublingual glands. \u0001 b. The parotid duct is also known as Wharton’s duct. \u0001 c. Stensen’s duct empties alongside the frenulum of the tongue. \u0001 d. Postganglionic parasympathetic fibres to the sub- mandibular gland are transmitted via the chorda tympani nerve. \u0001 e. The submandibular gland is related to the poster- ior border of mylohyoid.","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Thoracic Wall and Ribs","topicOriginal":"Thoracic Wall and Ribs","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Thoracic Wall and Ribs","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-d980ff0d59bc","stem":"20 Which statement about the surface anatomy of the neck is true? \u0001 a. The hard palate lies at the same level as the atlas. \u0001 b. The hyoid lies at the level of C4. \u0001 c. The cricoid lies at the level of C5. \u0001 d. The oesophagus commences at the level of C7. \u0001 e. The carotid tubercle lies at the level of C3.","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Mediastinum","topicOriginal":"Mediastinum","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Mediastinum","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-0a7b6283a5c9","stem":"21 Which of the following statements about the anterior triangle of the neck is incorrect? \u0001 a. Lies between the midline, the mandible and the anterior border of sternocleidomastoid. \u0001 b. Is further divided by the digastric and omohyoid muscles. \u0001 c. Contains the submandibular gland. \u0001 d. Contains the external jugular vein. \u0001 e. Contains the carotid sheath. 136 HEAD AND NECK: QUESTIONS","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Head and Neck","topicOriginal":"Head and Neck","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Head and Neck","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-f4b2e6c2c98d","stem":"22 Which of these statements about the posterior triangle of the neck is incorrect? \u0001 a. It is bounded by the sternocleidomastoid, trapez- ius and the middle third of the clavicle. \u0001 b. It contains the spinal accessory nerve. \u0001 c. The spinal accessory nerve can be identified at the posterior border of sternocleidomastoid. \u0001 d. It is floored by prevertebral fascia. \u0001 e. It contains the first part of the subclavian artery.","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Head and Neck · Suggested","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":"Head and Neck","options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Head and Neck · Suggested","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-183b7a0da10c","stem":"23 Which statement about the fascial planes of the neck is not correct? \u0001 a. The investing layer of fascia completely sur- rounds the neck. \u0001 b. The investing layer of fascia contributes to the capsule of the parotid gland. \u0001 c. The prevertebral fascia is continuous with the axillary sheath. \u0001 d. The pretracheal fascia has two layers. \u0001 e. The prevertebral fascia merges with the pericardium.","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Cardiovascular Anatomy","topicOriginal":"Cardiovascular Anatomy","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Cardiovascular Anatomy","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-983763b5e64d","stem":"24 Which statement concerning the muscles of the neck is true? \u0001 a. The tendon of omohyoid overlies the internal jugular vein. \u0001 b. The suprahyoid muscles are supplied by the ansa cervicalis. \u0001 c. Sternothyroid lies superficial to sternohyoid. \u0001 d. Stylohyoid is supplied by the glossopharyngeal nerve. \u0001 e. Mylohyoid is supplied by the facial nerve. 137 HEAD AND NECK: QUESTIONS","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Head and Neck","topicOriginal":"Head and Neck","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Head and Neck","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-243faa5a8169","stem":"25 Which of the following statements describing the phar- ynx is incorrect? \u0001 a. The nasopharynx derives its sensory innervation from the trigeminal nerve. \u0001 b. The glossopharyngeal nerve passes between the superior and middle constrictors. \u0001 c. It is lined by stratified squamous epithelium throughout. \u0001 d. The weakest part of the muscular wall is at the level of the inferior constrictor. \u0001 e. It ends at the level of C6.","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Thoracic Wall and Ribs","topicOriginal":"Thoracic Wall and Ribs","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Thoracic Wall and Ribs","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-0d765c198250","stem":"26 Which of the following statements about the larynx is incorrect? \u0001 a. The laryngeal inlet opens into the laryngopharynx. \u0001 b. The vestibule of the larynx lies between the ves- tibular fold and the vocal fold. \u0001 c. The narrowest part of the larynx in an adult is at the level of the vocal folds. \u0001 d. The cricothyroid membrane forms the vocal ligament. \u0001 e. The infraglottic portion is supplied by the inferior thyroid artery.","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Head and Neck · Suggested","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":"Head and Neck","options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Head and Neck · Suggested","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-0a48b3426a8c","stem":"27 Which statement, concerning the larynx is correct? \u0001 a. The supraglottic part of the larynx drains to the upper deep cervical nodes. \u0001 b. The vocal folds drain to the inferior deep cervical nodes. \u0001 c. The cricothyroid is the only muscle to abduct the vocal cords. \u0001 d. The posterior cricoarytenoid is supplied by the superior laryngeal nerve. \u0001 e. The vocal folds are cushioned by a layer of submucosa. 138 HEAD AND NECK: QUESTIONS","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Head and Neck · Suggested","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":"Head and Neck","options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Head and Neck · Suggested","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-56f5d28928fb","stem":"28 Which statement about the blood supply of the thyroid is correct? \u0001 a. Inferior thyroid artery is a branch of the external carotid artery. \u0001 b. Inferior thyroid artery normally supplies the superior and inferior parathyroids. \u0001 c. Thyroidea ima usually arises from the arch of the aorta. \u0001 d. Middle thyroid vein drains into the brachio- cephalic vein. \u0001 e. External laryngeal nerve is closely related to the inferior thyroid vessels.","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Head and Neck","topicOriginal":"Head and Neck","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Head and Neck","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-6c04ef240633","stem":"29 Which statement regarding the development of the thy- roid and parathyroids is not correct? \u0001 a. The thyroid is in its approximate adult position at birth. \u0001 b. The foramen caecum is an embryological rem- nant of the thyroid’s development. \u0001 c. Levator glandulae thyroidae is an embryological remnant of the thyroid’s development. \u0001 d. The inferior parathyroid glands develop from the 3rd branchial arch. \u0001 e. The superior parathyroids are more variable in position than the inferior parathyroids.","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Embryological Development","topicOriginal":"Embryological Development","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Embryological Development","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-a69cbcb10942","stem":"30 Which statement concerning the parathyroid glands is correct? \u0001 a. Four glands are present in 50% of subjects. \u0001 b. The superior parathyroid lies above the superior thyroid artery. \u0001 c. The parathyroids have a combined weight of 1g. \u0001 d. Oxyphil cells secrete parathyroid hormone. \u0001 e. The superior parathyroid lies within the pretra- cheal fascia. 139 HEAD AND NECK: QUESTIONS","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Head and Neck · Suggested","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":"Head and Neck","options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Head and Neck · Suggested","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-3d7672d59b07","stem":"31 Which statement best describes the external carotid artery and its branches? \u0001 a. The anterior part of the scalp is supplied by the facial branch of the external carotid. \u0001 b. The maxillary artery is divided into three parts by the lateral pterygoid muscle. \u0001 c. The facial artery is easily palpable at the anterior border of masseter. \u0001 d. The lingual artery gives the superior laryngeal artery as a branch. \u0001 e. The middle meningeal artery is a branch of the facial artery.","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Thoracic Wall and Ribs","topicOriginal":"Thoracic Wall and Ribs","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Thoracic Wall and Ribs","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-bd924cf78512","stem":"32 Which statement regarding the lymphatic drainage of the head and neck is incorrect? \u0001 a. All the lymph drainage from the face enters the deep cervical nodes. \u0001 b. The left deep cervical nodes drain into the thor- acic duct. \u0001 c. The right lymphatic duct drains into the junction between the right internal jugular and brachio- cephalic veins. \u0001 d. The superficial cervical nodes lie along the exter- nal jugular vein. \u0001 e. The jugulo-omohyoid node is easily palpable when enlarged. HEAD AND NECK: ANSWERS 140 ANSWERS","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Head and Neck · Suggested","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":"Head and Neck","options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Head and Neck · Suggested","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-fba20eb364ad","stem":"Write answer on the examination sheets provided for Multiple choice Questions and answer the rest on the spaces provided.","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-9a6e01c4bebb","stem":"This is a THREE-HOUR paper consisting of EIGHTEEN printed pages with FIVE sections. Section I : Multiple-Choice Questions Section II : Filling the gaps Section III : True or False Section IV : Matching items Section V : Labelling diagrams","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-ca9facb60879","stem":"Mark T for true and F for false statement regarding Astrocytes (10marks) I. Their foot processes attach on basement membrane of capillary endothelium in forming blood brain barrier ________ II. Supports blood capillaries _________ III. Are phagocytic in the CNS _________ IV. Their nuclei form the brain nuclei _________ V. Myelinate axons of both the CNS and ANS _________ VI. Are associated with support and nutrition __________ VII. Forms ganglionic cells in the sympathetic ganglia __________ VIII. Are only found in the gray matter in the cerebellum __________ IX. Guides migration of neurons during embryonic development. _______ X. Their foot like processes forms scar on damaged brain tissue hindering axon regeneration. __________ SECTION IV MATCHING QUESTIONS","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Embryological Development","topicOriginal":"Embryological Development","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Embryological Development","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-5cc5340c7bf1","stem":"At what level of the vascular tree does gas exchange occur?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Capillary"},{"label":"B","text":"Arteriole"},{"label":"C","text":"Venule"},{"label":"D","text":"Elastic artery"},{"label":"E","text":"Muscular artery Capillaries are very thin walled in order to easily allow the exchange of gases. Gaseous exchange between the blood and tissues occurs at the level of the capillaries. Arterioles are small branches of arteries with only one or two layers of smooth muscle in the tunica media. Arterioles regulate the amount of blood going into the capillary bed. Venules are small branches of veins. Elastic arteries are the arteries leaving the heart and the major branches. The aorta is an elastic artery. Most of the named arteries are muscular arteries (with the exception of the aorta and the major branches off the aorta). The dividing line between elastic arteries and muscular arteries is not clear cut. However, a pronounced internal elastic membrane and external elastic membrane are distinguishing characteristics of muscular arteries. Histology hint from Sarah Bellham: Elastic arteries also have an internal elastic membrane. However, there is so much elastic material in the tunica intima of an elastic artery, that a single, discrete internal elastic membrane is not visible."}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":true,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["long_option_manual_review","repeated_question_stem","topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[],"duplicateStemGroup":"a203194bd0afb2c357b975064a700449613ba02f"},{"id":"q-9aa337ebe5ff","stem":"Which layer in an artery is primarily skeletal muscle?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Tunica intima"},{"label":"B","text":"Tunica media"},{"label":"C","text":"Tunica externa"},{"label":"D","text":"All of the above"},{"label":"E","text":"None of the above The tunica intima is the innermost layer of a blood vessel. It is lined by endothelium The tunica media is the middle layer of a blood vessel. The tunica media is primarily smooth muscle. The tunica externa or tunica adventitia is the outer layer of a blood vessel. In large vessels, the tunica adventitia contains vasa vasorum (blood vessels) and nervi vascularis (nerves)."}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":true,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["repeated_question_stem","topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[],"duplicateStemGroup":"e3b5c017b19be359622a5f90718c0d954b4d54a7"},{"id":"q-0826bd590d65","stem":"Which of the following is NOT a distinguishing feature between larger veins and arteries?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Veins have valves whereas arteries do not have valves"},{"label":"B","text":"The tunics in veins are not as clearly delimited as are the tunics in arteries"},{"label":"C","text":"The walls in veins are thinner than the walls in arteries"},{"label":"D","text":"The lumen of a vein is smaller than the lumen of an artery"},{"label":"E","text":"None. All of the above are true Veins have valves whereas arteries do not have valves. The tunics in veins are not as clearly delimited as are the tunics in arteries. The walls in veins are thinner than the walls in arteries. The lumen of a vein is larger than the lumen of an artery."}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":true,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["repeated_question_stem","topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[],"duplicateStemGroup":"360163f51e124f99f3f16f41a1f28e5579d53360"},{"id":"q-c61a0e75f84f","stem":"In which structure are things moved across the epithelium via pinocytotic vesicles?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Continuous capillaries"},{"label":"B","text":"Fenestrated capillaries"},{"label":"C","text":"Sinusoidal capillaries"},{"label":"D","text":"AV anastomoses"},{"label":"E","text":"Venous sinus A characteristic of continuous capillaries is that things are transported across the epithelium via pinocytotic vesicles. A characteristic of fenestrated capillaries is the presence of pores or fenestrae. Sinusoidal capillaries (sinusoids) are wide leaky capillaries. They are found in the liver, spleen, and bone marrow. An arteriovenous anastomoses (AV anastomoses or AV shunt) is a direct route between arteries and veins. It bypasses the capillary bed. A venous sinus is a venous space lined by endothelium. A venous sinus surrounding the brain exists which is called the dural sinus."}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":true,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["long_option_manual_review","repeated_question_stem","topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[],"duplicateStemGroup":"01a01cd18a7e66d96a62edf6497e67d2981b3a67"},{"id":"q-7df75755229e","stem":"What is a thoroughfare which is a an intermediate between an arteriole and capillary?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Metcapillary"},{"label":"B","text":"Metartery c. Metvenule"},{"label":"D","text":"Metarteriole"},{"label":"E","text":"None of the above A metarteriole is a thoroughfare that can be considered an intermediate between an arteriole and capillary is a metarteriole."}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["repeated_question_stem","topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[],"duplicateStemGroup":"e4b2b166e6414005cce4829223818aaa65b0ab14"},{"id":"q-99104f8cfa7e","stem":"Which layer in an artery contains the endothelium?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Tunica intima"},{"label":"B","text":"Tunica media"},{"label":"C","text":"Tunica externa"},{"label":"D","text":"All of the above"},{"label":"E","text":"None of the above The tunica intima is the innermost layer of a blood vessel. It is lined by endothelium The tunica media is the middle layer of a blood vessel. The tunica media is primarily smooth muscle. The tunica externa or tunica adventitia is the outer layer of a blood vessel. In large vessels, the tunica adventitia contains vasa vasorum (blood vessels) and nervi vascularis (nerves)."}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":true,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["repeated_question_stem","topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[],"duplicateStemGroup":"dc83e0df9e3185761012712cb82232087ebc9195"},{"id":"q-abf63a80c531","stem":"What do you call the simple squamous epithelium that lines the blood vessels?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Epithelioid tissue"},{"label":"B","text":"Mesothelium"},{"label":"C","text":"Endothelium"},{"label":"D","text":"Transitional"},{"label":"E","text":"Pseudostratified Epithelial tissue has cells that are very tightly packed together. There is always a free surface associated with epithelial tissue. If a tissue is composed of a conglomeration of cells in tightly packed together, but it does not have a free surface, the tissue is called epithelioid tissue. An example of epithelioid tissue is the parenchyma of the adrenal gland. Mesothelium is simple squamous epithelium that lines the abdominal cavity, the pericardial cavity, and the thoracic cavity. Endothelium is simple squamous epithelium that lines the vascular system. Transitional epithelium is seen in the urinary tract. Transitional epithelium has dome shaped cells on the apical surface. Pseudostratified epithelium is a type of epithelium that has cells which all touch the basement membrane. Pseudostratified epithelium is only one cell layer thick. Pseudostratified epithelium appears stratified, but it is not really stratified. Thus the name. The prefix \"pseudo\" means false, such as pseudonym or pseudo-science."}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":true,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["long_option_manual_review","repeated_question_stem","topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[],"duplicateStemGroup":"6243bd3ea9e6385dcc2c4abb6eb5cc3f4ba10414"},{"id":"q-a28f5a6b4aea","stem":"What is the brachial artery?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Upper Limb","topicOriginal":"Upper Limb","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Capillary"},{"label":"B","text":"Arteriole"},{"label":"C","text":"Venule"},{"label":"D","text":"Elastic artery"},{"label":"E","text":"Muscular artery Capillaries are very thin walled in order to easily allow the exchange of gases. Gaseous exchange between the blood and tissues occurs at the level of the capillaries. Arterioles are small branches of arteries with only one or two layers of smooth muscle in the tunica media. Arterioles regulate the amount of blood going into the capillary bed. Venules are small branches of veins. Elastic arteries are the arteries leaving the heart and the major branches. The aorta is an elastic artery. Most of the named arteries are muscular arteries (with the exception of the aorta and the major branches off the aorta). The dividing line between elastic arteries and muscular arteries is not clear cut. However, a pronounced internal elastic membrane and external elastic membrane are distinguishing characteristics of muscular arteries. Histology hint from Sarah Bellham: Elastic arteries also have an internal elastic membrane. However, there is so much elastic material in the tunica intima of an elastic artery, that a single, discrete internal elastic membrane is not visible."}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":true,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Upper Limb","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["long_option_manual_review","repeated_question_stem"],"cleaningOperations":[],"duplicateStemGroup":"b329d1b42d4c441a38bfbcb5bc40ef030fdc93db"},{"id":"q-14f66e9deeed","stem":"Which of the following is a distinct structure found specifically in the liver, spleen, and bone marrow?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Abdomen and GI Anatomy · Suggested","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":"Abdomen and GI Anatomy","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Continuous capillaries"},{"label":"B","text":"Fenestrated capillaries"},{"label":"C","text":"Sinusoidal capillaries"},{"label":"D","text":"AV anastomoses"},{"label":"E","text":"Venous sinus A characteristic of continuous capillaries is that things are transported across the epithelium via pinocytotic vesicles. A characteristic of fenestrated capillaries is the presence of pores or fenestrae. Sinusoidal capillaries (sinusoids) are wide leaky capillaries. They are found in the liver, spleen, and bone marrow. An arteriovenous anastomoses (AV anastomoses or AV shunt) is a direct route between arteries and veins. It bypasses the capillary bed. A venous sinus is a venous space lined by endothelium. A venous sinus surrounding the brain exists which is called the dural sinus."}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":true,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Abdomen and GI Anatomy · Suggested","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["long_option_manual_review","repeated_question_stem","topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[],"duplicateStemGroup":"5322e2db439c7eb565750a73e5c2d66a410ad024"},{"id":"q-7767f3597a2c","stem":"Which layer in an artery is primarily smooth muscle?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Tunica intima"},{"label":"B","text":"Tunica media"},{"label":"C","text":"Tunica externa"},{"label":"D","text":"All of the above"},{"label":"E","text":"None of the above The tunica intima is the innermost layer of a blood vessel. It is lined by endothelium The tunica media is the middle layer of a blood vessel. The tunica media is primarily smooth muscle. The tunica externa or tunica adventitia is the outer layer of a blood vessel. In large vessels, the tunica adventitia contains vasa vasorum (blood vessels) and nervi vascularis (nerves)."}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":true,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["repeated_question_stem","topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[],"duplicateStemGroup":"dc778560596c3d64c3e1948988081192971108d9"},{"id":"q-af5600267b75","stem":"A pronounced internal elastic membrane and external elastic membrane are distinguishing characteristics of which type of vessel?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Capillary"},{"label":"B","text":"Arteriole"},{"label":"C","text":"Venule"},{"label":"D","text":"Elastic artery"},{"label":"E","text":"Muscular artery Capillaries are very thin walled in order to easily allow the exchange of gases. Gaseous exchange between the blood and tissues occurs at the level of the capillaries. Arterioles are small branches of arteries with only one or two layers of smooth muscle in the tunica media. Arterioles regulate the amount of blood going into the capillary bed. Venules are small branches of veins. Elastic arteries are the arteries leaving the heart and the major branches. The aorta is an elastic artery. Most of the named arteries are muscular arteries (with the exception of the aorta and the major branches off the aorta). The dividing line between elastic arteries and muscular arteries is not clear cut. However, a pronounced internal elastic membrane and external elastic membrane are distinguishing characteristics of muscular arteries. Histology hint from Sarah Bellham: Elastic arteries also have an internal elastic membrane. However, there is so much elastic material in the tunica intima of an elastic artery, that a single, discrete internal elastic membrane is not visible."}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":true,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["long_option_manual_review","repeated_question_stem","topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[],"duplicateStemGroup":"5c91415627866c7274d145f4fb206aea8ea96949"},{"id":"q-fa0e537370fa","stem":"Which of the following constitutes the microvascular bed of a tissue?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Capillaries"},{"label":"B","text":"Capillaries and arterioles"},{"label":"C","text":"Capillaries, arterioles, and post capillary venules"},{"label":"D","text":"Capillaries, arterioles, post capillary venules, and veins"},{"label":"E","text":"Capillaries, arterioles, post capillary venules, veins, and arteries a tissue."}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":true,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["repeated_question_stem","topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[],"duplicateStemGroup":"84407433d3cf9fd16793c2d5aa2b438258cd05aa"},{"id":"q-02fa78cc2eda","stem":"What are wide, leaky capillaries called?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Continuous capillaries"},{"label":"B","text":"Fenestrated capillaries"},{"label":"C","text":"Sinusoidal capillaries"},{"label":"D","text":"AV anastomoses"},{"label":"E","text":"Venous sinus A characteristic of continuous capillaries is that things are transported across the epithelium via pinocytotic vesicles. A characteristic of fenestrated capillaries is the presence of pores or fenestrae. Sinusoidal capillaries (sinusoids) are wide leaky capillaries. They are found in the liver, spleen, and bone marrow. An arteriovenous anastomoses (AV anastomoses or AV shunt) is a direct route between arteries and veins. It bypasses the capillary bed. A venous sinus is a venous space lined by endothelium. A venous sinus surrounding the brain exists which is called the dural sinus."}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":true,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["long_option_manual_review","repeated_question_stem","topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[],"duplicateStemGroup":"cf62860724c31198a7f83fe66a3b3fa439ee7ee0"},{"id":"q-bb9bad575153","stem":"What is a direct route between arteries and veins called?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Continuous capillaries"},{"label":"B","text":"Fenestrated capillaries"},{"label":"C","text":"Sinusoidal capillaries"},{"label":"D","text":"AV anastomoses"},{"label":"E","text":"Venous sinus A characteristic of continuous capillaries is that things are transported across the epithelium via pinocytotic vesicles. A characteristic of fenestrated capillaries is the presence of pores or fenestrae. Sinusoidal capillaries (sinusoids) are wide leaky capillaries. They are found in the liver, spleen, and bone marrow. An arteriovenous anastomoses (AV anastomoses or AV shunt) is a direct route between arteries and veins. It bypasses the capillary bed. A venous sinus is a venous space lined by endothelium. A venous sinus surrounding the brain exists which is called the dural sinus."}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":true,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["long_option_manual_review","repeated_question_stem","topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[],"duplicateStemGroup":"6af8386173de8952f05857d809e234cfbf8d4fe2"},{"id":"q-58b5aff4539a","stem":"In which of the following is an arterial portal system found?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Kidney"},{"label":"B","text":"Liver"},{"label":"C","text":"Muscle"},{"label":"D","text":"Brain"},{"label":"E","text":"Stomach The normal flow of blood is as follows: artery - arteriole - capillary - post capillary venule -vein. However, exceptions to this pattern of blood flow exist. The phenomenon when a vein is between two capillary beds is called a venous portal system. An example of this is the hepatic portal system. Another example of a venous portal system is seen in the brain between the hypothalamus and pituitary. The phenomenon when an arteriole is between two capillary beds is called an arterial portal system. This is seen in the kidney."}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":true,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["long_option_manual_review","repeated_question_stem","topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[],"duplicateStemGroup":"507c231c7a4fecc715f29c888636ac81e4b6a4c0"},{"id":"q-e2e6e1d9a9b1","stem":"What are vasa vasorum?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Vasoactive material"},{"label":"B","text":"Valves"},{"label":"C","text":"Vasopressin secreting cells"},{"label":"D","text":"Nerves"},{"label":"E","text":"Blood vessels Vasa vasorum are the blood vessels of the blood vessels. These are the vessels which supply the vessel wall."}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":true,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["repeated_question_stem","topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[],"duplicateStemGroup":"5734cc3003428884a7dc2be29e8e0ae1a95ec9f1"},{"id":"q-b1c5db008bee","stem":"Which structure has only a few layers of muscle in the tunica media?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Capillary"},{"label":"B","text":"Arteriole"},{"label":"C","text":"Elastic artery"},{"label":"D","text":"Muscular artery The tunica media is the middle layer of a blood vessel. The tunica media is primarily smooth muscle."}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":true,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["repeated_question_stem","topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[],"duplicateStemGroup":"834625728dfe1c043a07c8ab8f26b8a37468fe1b"},{"id":"q-bde13df7c7c4","stem":"Which layer in a large vessel contains the nervi vascularis?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Tunica intima"},{"label":"B","text":"Tunica media"},{"label":"C","text":"Tunica externa"},{"label":"D","text":"All of the above"},{"label":"E","text":"None of the above The tunica intima is the innermost layer of a blood vessel. It is lined by endothelium The tunica media is the middle layer of a blood vessel. The tunica media is primarily smooth muscle. The tunica externa or tunica adventitia is the outer layer of a blood vessel. In large vessels, the tunica adventitia contains vasa vasorum (blood vessels) and nervi vascularis (nerves)."}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":true,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["repeated_question_stem","topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[],"duplicateStemGroup":"c25e9affc4a5d417287973bb8467df2c5420e3c4"},{"id":"q-a9c976bf246f","stem":"What vessel regulates the amount of blood going into a capillary bed?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Capillary"},{"label":"B","text":"Arteriole"},{"label":"C","text":"Venule"},{"label":"D","text":"Elastic artery"},{"label":"E","text":"Muscular artery Capillaries are very thin walled in order to easily allow the exchange of gases. Gaseous exchange between the blood and tissues occurs at the level of the capillaries. Arterioles are small branches of arteries with only one or two layers of smooth muscle in the tunica media. Arterioles regulate the amount of blood going into the capillary bed. Venules are small branches of veins. Elastic arteries are the arteries leaving the heart and the major branches. The aorta is an elastic artery. Most of the named arteries are muscular arteries (with the exception of the aorta and the major branches off the aorta). The dividing line between elastic arteries and muscular arteries is not clear cut. However, a pronounced internal elastic membrane and external elastic membrane are distinguishing characteristics of muscular arteries. Histology hint from Sarah Bellham: Elastic arteries also have an internal elastic membrane. However, there is so much elastic material in the tunica intima of an elastic artery, that a single, discrete internal elastic membrane is not visible."}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":true,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["long_option_manual_review","repeated_question_stem","topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[],"duplicateStemGroup":"bab0954fbbe402fa2b040d3b810fa1ee5422b403"},{"id":"q-a4c6647d7451","stem":"What is the aorta?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Capillary"},{"label":"B","text":"Arteriole"},{"label":"C","text":"Venule"},{"label":"D","text":"Elastic artery"},{"label":"E","text":"Muscular artery Capillaries are very thin walled in order to easily allow the exchange of gases. Gaseous exchange between the blood and tissues occurs at the level of the capillaries. Arterioles are small branches of arteries with only one or two layers of smooth muscle in the tunica media. Arterioles regulate the amount of blood going into the capillary bed. Venules are small branches of veins. Elastic arteries are the arteries leaving the heart and the major branches. The aorta is an elastic artery. Most of the named arteries are muscular arteries (with the exception of the aorta and the major branches off the aorta). The dividing line between elastic arteries and muscular arteries is not clear cut. However, a pronounced internal elastic membrane and external elastic membrane are distinguishing characteristics of muscular arteries. Histology hint from Sarah Bellham: Elastic arteries also have an internal elastic membrane. However, there is so much elastic material in the tunica intima of an elastic artery, that a single, discrete internal elastic membrane is not visible."}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":true,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["long_option_manual_review","repeated_question_stem","topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[],"duplicateStemGroup":"6c3bc20bb4c1ec4ad4e2eecc1766a6fdfc83f224"},{"id":"q-db0bd9649b9b","stem":"What are most of the named arteries in the body?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Capillary"},{"label":"B","text":"Arteriole"},{"label":"C","text":"Venule"},{"label":"D","text":"Elastic artery"},{"label":"E","text":"Muscular artery Capillaries are very thin walled in order to easily allow the exchange of gases. Gaseous exchange between the blood and tissues occurs at the level of the capillaries. Arterioles are small branches of arteries with only one or two layers of smooth muscle in the tunica media. Arterioles regulate the amount of blood going into the capillary bed. Venules are small branches of veins. Elastic arteries are the arteries leaving the heart and the major branches. The aorta is an elastic artery. Most of the named arteries are muscular arteries (with the exception of the aorta and the major branches off the aorta). The dividing line between elastic arteries and muscular arteries is not clear cut. However, a pronounced internal elastic membrane and external elastic membrane are distinguishing characteristics of muscular arteries. Histology hint from Sarah Bellham: Elastic arteries also have an internal elastic membrane. However, there is so much elastic material in the tunica intima of an elastic artery, that a single, discrete internal elastic membrane is not visible."}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":true,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["long_option_manual_review","repeated_question_stem","topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[],"duplicateStemGroup":"986c6a27c66419a8e4bccdaac77155f45a8b00f0"},{"id":"q-0e6d15368a16","stem":"Which structure contains pores?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Continuous capillaries"},{"label":"B","text":"Fenestrated capillaries"},{"label":"C","text":"Sinusoidal capillaries"},{"label":"D","text":"AV anastomoses"},{"label":"E","text":"Venous sinus A characteristic of continuous capillaries is that things are transported across the epithelium via pinocytotic vesicles. A characteristic of fenestrated capillaries is the presence of pores or fenestrae. Sinusoidal capillaries (sinusoids) are wide leaky capillaries. They are found in the liver, spleen, and bone marrow. An arteriovenous anastomoses (AV anastomoses or AV shunt) is a direct route between arteries and veins. It bypasses the capillary bed. A venous sinus is a venous space lined by endothelium. A venous sinus surrounding the brain exists which is called the dural sinus."}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":true,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["long_option_manual_review","repeated_question_stem","topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[],"duplicateStemGroup":"a21e92a89626440ab00c6228029abbab98024d5f"},{"id":"q-9daff702fe6f","stem":"Which layer in an artery is also called the tunica adventitia?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Tunica intima"},{"label":"B","text":"Tunica media"},{"label":"C","text":"Tunica externa"},{"label":"D","text":"All of the above"},{"label":"E","text":"None of the above The tunica intima is the innermost layer of a blood vessel. It is lined by endothelium The tunica media is the middle layer of a blood vessel. The tunica media is primarily smooth muscle. The tunica externa or tunica adventitia is the outer layer of a blood vessel. In large vessels, the tunica adventitia contains vasa vasorum (blood vessels) and nervi vascularis (nerves)."}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":true,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["repeated_question_stem","topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[],"duplicateStemGroup":"07b22fcf30de068c307ac35f25cb2597b58d7400"},{"id":"q-e79e0a5bbc9f","stem":"Which layer in an artery is primarily connective tissue?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Tunica intima"},{"label":"B","text":"Tunica media"},{"label":"C","text":"Tunica externa"},{"label":"D","text":"All of the above"},{"label":"E","text":"None of the above The tunica intima is the innermost layer of a blood vessel. It is lined by endothelium The tunica media is the middle layer of a blood vessel. The tunica media is primarily smooth muscle. The tunica externa or tunica adventitia is the outer layer of a blood vessel. In large vessels, the tunica adventitia contains vasa vasorum (blood vessels) and nervi vascularis (nerves)."}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":true,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["repeated_question_stem","topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[],"duplicateStemGroup":"95c1e9746fe33ca0a9856c68cb71fa8ce910c2e5"},{"id":"q-bf2e0c4a0cb9","stem":"What type of tissue lines blood vessels?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Simple squamous epithelium"},{"label":"B","text":"Simple cuboidal epithelium"},{"label":"C","text":"Simple columnar epithelium"},{"label":"D","text":"Stratified squamous epithelium"},{"label":"E","text":"Transitional epithelium The lining of a blood vessel is simple squamous epithelium. This lining is called endothelium. Epithelium lines body cavities and surfaces. Simple squamous epithelium is \"simple\" because it is one cell thick. \"Squamous\" refers to the fact that the cells are flat."}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":true,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["repeated_question_stem","topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[],"duplicateStemGroup":"ac61fb0419345fc25e801ebf9f0733a2ca941d6c"},{"id":"q-c008ac09164a","stem":"In which of the following is a venous portal system found?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Kidney"},{"label":"B","text":"Liver"},{"label":"C","text":"Muscle"},{"label":"D","text":"Skin"},{"label":"E","text":"Stomach The normal flow of blood is as follows: artery - arteriole - capillary - post capillary venule -vein. However, exceptions to this pattern of blood flow exist. The phenomenon when a vein is between two capillary beds is called a venous portal system. An example of this is the hepatic portal system. Another example of a venous portal system is seen in the brain between the hypothalamus and pituitary. The phenomenon when an arteriole is between two capillary beds is called an arterial portal system. This is seen in the kidney."}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":true,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["long_option_manual_review","repeated_question_stem","topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[],"duplicateStemGroup":"b5d9adb863a48f210064871cdc0d7528e7004ca9"},{"id":"q-0a675de2501b","stem":"What are nervi vascularis?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Neuropil"},{"label":"B","text":"Neuroglia"},{"label":"C","text":"Pigmented lesion of a vessel"},{"label":"D","text":"Nerves"},{"label":"E","text":"Blood vessels Answer: d Nervi vascularis are nerves of the blood vessels. These are the nerves which supply the vessel wall."}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["repeated_question_stem","topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[],"duplicateStemGroup":"f6d22615017041b722d275c22fa82eff0e043c50"},{"id":"q-4c82548fda46","stem":"Which structure receives blood from the capillary bed?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Capillary"},{"label":"B","text":"Arteriole"},{"label":"C","text":"Venule"},{"label":"D","text":"Elastic artery"},{"label":"E","text":"Muscular artery Capillaries are very thin walled in order to easily allow the exchange of gases. Gaseous exchange between the blood and tissues occurs at the level of the capillaries. Arterioles are small branches of arteries with only one or two layers of smooth muscle in the tunica media. Arterioles regulate the amount of blood going into the capillary bed. Venules are small branches of veins. Elastic arteries are the arteries leaving the heart and the major branches. The aorta is an elastic artery. Most of the named arteries are muscular arteries (with the exception of the aorta and the major branches off the aorta). The dividing line between elastic arteries and muscular arteries is not clear cut. However, a pronounced internal elastic membrane and external elastic membrane are distinguishing characteristics of muscular arteries. Histology hint from Sarah Bellham: Elastic arteries also have an internal elastic membrane. However, there is so much elastic material in the tunica intima of an elastic artery, that a single, discrete internal elastic membrane is not visible."}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":true,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["long_option_manual_review","repeated_question_stem","topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[],"duplicateStemGroup":"09901a9959bec3bae3d731f82227f9c21ceae594"},{"id":"q-f2c70be0d060","stem":"Which layer in a large artery or vein contains the vasa vasorum?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Tunica intima"},{"label":"B","text":"Tunica media"},{"label":"C","text":"Tunica externa"},{"label":"D","text":"All of the above"},{"label":"E","text":"None of the above The tunica intima is the innermost layer of a blood vessel. It is lined by endothelium The tunica media is the middle layer of a blood vessel. The tunica media is primarily smooth muscle. The tunica externa or tunica adventitia is the outer layer of a blood vessel. In large vessels, the tunica adventitia contains vasa vasorum (blood vessels) and nervi vascularis (nerves)."}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":true,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["repeated_question_stem","topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[],"duplicateStemGroup":"a761c468d95e05d793c4aa56d9a499dfa9b8fcf3"},{"id":"q-f74868b4fd13","stem":"What is the venous channel which is around the brain?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Continuous capillaries"},{"label":"B","text":"Fenestrated capillaries"},{"label":"C","text":"Sinusoidal capillaries"},{"label":"D","text":"AV Anastomoses"},{"label":"E","text":"Venous sinus A characteristic of continuous capillaries is that things are transported across the epithelium via pinocytotic vesicles. A characteristic of fenestrated capillaries is the presence of pores or fenestrae. Sinusoidal capillaries (sinusoids) are wide leaky capillaries. They are found in the liver, spleen, and bone marrow. An arteriovenous anastomoses (AV anastomoses or AV shunt) is a direct route between arteries and veins. It bypasses the capillary bed. A venous sinus is a venous space lined by endothelium. A venous sinus surrounding the brain exists which is called the dural sinus."}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":true,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["long_option_manual_review","repeated_question_stem","topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[],"duplicateStemGroup":"f4a828afa3b2f302f681915eaea7a42cf55fa76a"},{"id":"q-25195e98fcde","stem":"What is another term for lymphatic nodules?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Lymph follicles"},{"label":"B","text":"White pulp"},{"label":"C","text":"Peyer's patches"},{"label":"D","text":"Lymph node"},{"label":"E","text":"Diffuse lymphatic tissue The localized concentrations of lymphocytes that are seen in the respiratory tract, genitourinary tract, and gastrointestinal tract are lymph follicles. They are also called lymphatic nodules. The lymphatic tissue in the spleen is called white pulp. The large aggregates of lymphatic tissue in the ileum are called Peyer's patches. A lymph node is an encapsulated lymphatic organ. The random distribution of lymphocytes seen in the lamina propria of the respiratory tract, genitourinary tract, and gastrointestinal tract is called diffuse lymphatic tissue. Difuse lymphatic tissue in not encapsulated."}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":true,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["long_option_manual_review","repeated_question_stem","topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[],"duplicateStemGroup":"6d21a8e79ae6a2204ec959d8259a2d4d80871873"},{"id":"q-59ab0f0c0bd2","stem":"Which layer of the gastrointestinal tract contains the gut associated lymphatic tissue?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Mucosa"},{"label":"B","text":"Sub mucosa"},{"label":"C","text":"Muscularis externa"},{"label":"D","text":"Serosa"},{"label":"E","text":"Adventitia The mucosa is the innermost layer of the GI tract. The mucosa consists of a lining epithelium, lamina propria and muscularis mucosae. Gut associated lymphatic tissue (GALT) is found in the mucosa and sometimes extends into the submucosa."}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":true,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["repeated_question_stem","topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[],"duplicateStemGroup":"8e88bb43105d0df52edb16e22c6665a1a0ff49ce"},{"id":"q-30ff6da5519a","stem":"What is the acronym for the diffuse lymphatic tissue found in the intestinal tract?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"BALT"},{"label":"B","text":"DALT"},{"label":"C","text":"FALT"},{"label":"D","text":"GALT"},{"label":"E","text":"HALT Diffuse lymphatic tissue is non-encapsulated lymphatic tissue. It is found in the gastrointestinal tract, the genito-urinary tract, and the respiratory tract. In the gastrointestinal tract it is referred to as GALT (gut associated lymphatic tissue). In the respiratory tract it is referred to as BALT (bronchi associated lymphatic tissue)."}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":true,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["repeated_question_stem","topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[],"duplicateStemGroup":"bc45bb5ff69cc11c00cf25dc893f888c7e90af13"},{"id":"q-bed08130f31f","stem":"What is another name for a splenic nodule?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Malpighian corpuscle"},{"label":"B","text":"Trabeculae"},{"label":"C","text":"White pulp"},{"label":"D","text":"Red pulp"},{"label":"E","text":"Cords of Billroth The spleen has a connective tissue capsule. The invaginations of the capsule into the splenic parenchyma are trabeculae. The parenchyma of the spleen can be divided into the white pulp and the red pulp. The white pulp of the spleen is the lymphatic portion of the spleen. Within the white pulp, splenic nodules are found. Splenic nodules are also called Malpighian corpuscles. The red pulp is made up of the splenic sinuses and splenic cords. The splenic cords are also called the cords of Billroth."}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":true,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["long_option_manual_review","repeated_question_stem","topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[],"duplicateStemGroup":"a5c1f5fbf88ab0224f1ed1dae6ecab311ad285f0"},{"id":"q-4d513cf4f404","stem":"What is the term for the entire lymphatic region of the spleen?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Abdomen and GI Anatomy · Suggested","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":"Abdomen and GI Anatomy","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Malpighian corpuscle"},{"label":"B","text":"Trabeculae"},{"label":"C","text":"White pulp"},{"label":"D","text":"Red pulp"},{"label":"E","text":"Cords of Billroth The spleen has a connective tissue capsule. The invaginations of the capsule into the splenic parenchyma are trabeculae. The parenchyma of the spleen can be divided into the white pulp and the red pulp. The white pulp of the spleen is the lymphatic portion of the spleen. Within the white pulp, splenic nodules are found. Splenic nodules are also called Malpighian corpuscles. The red pulp is made up of the splenic sinuses and splenic cords. The splenic cords are also called the cords of Billroth."}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":true,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Abdomen and GI Anatomy · Suggested","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["long_option_manual_review","repeated_question_stem","topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[],"duplicateStemGroup":"34643d5af47488fdf1a593177a87b2d540833666"},{"id":"q-bd628db7617f","stem":"Which of the following is NOT a function of the spleen?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Abdomen and GI Anatomy · Suggested","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":"Abdomen and GI Anatomy","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Destruction of red blood cells"},{"label":"B","text":"Lymphocyte production"},{"label":"C","text":"Storage of blood"},{"label":"D","text":"Fetal blood cell formation"},{"label":"E","text":"All of the above are functions of the spleen Answer: e The spleen is involved in destruction of old or damaged red blood cells. Storage of blood occurs in the spleen. In the fetus, the spleen is involved in blood cell formation. Lymphocyte and antibody production occurs in the spleen."}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Abdomen and GI Anatomy · Suggested","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["repeated_question_stem","topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[],"duplicateStemGroup":"23409b8a6277d23d921bcce6e6a3ba96bfdcb5e6"},{"id":"q-e41bbf0641c9","stem":"What do you call the random distribution of lymphocytes that are found in the respiratory tract, genitourinary tract, and gastrointestinal tract?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Thoracic Wall and Ribs","topicOriginal":"Thoracic Wall and Ribs","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Lymph follicles"},{"label":"B","text":"White pulp"},{"label":"C","text":"Peyer's patches"},{"label":"D","text":"Lymph node"},{"label":"E","text":"Diffuse lymphatic tissue The localized concentrations of lymphocytes that are seen in the respiratory tract, genitourinary tract, and gastrointestinal tract are lymph follicles. They are also called lymphatic nodules. The lymphatic tissue in the spleen is called white pulp. The large aggregates of lymphatic tissue in the ileum are called Peyer's patches. A lymph node is an encapsulated lymphatic organ. The random distribution of lymphocytes seen in the lamina propria of the respiratory tract, genitourinary tract, and gastrointestinal tract is called diffuse lymphatic tissue. Difuse lymphatic tissue in not encapsulated."}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":true,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Thoracic Wall and Ribs","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["long_option_manual_review","repeated_question_stem"],"cleaningOperations":[],"duplicateStemGroup":"aaa03332b3fd67cd52c8955dd07106e77db92ceb"},{"id":"q-61e126693569","stem":"What is a characteristic of a secondary nodule?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Germinal center"},{"label":"B","text":"Lymphocytes"},{"label":"C","text":"Capsule"},{"label":"D","text":"Trabeculae"},{"label":"E","text":"None of the above A secondary lymphatic nodule is characterized by the presence of a germinal center."}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":true,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["repeated_question_stem","topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[],"duplicateStemGroup":"a59a1c7a7c6bfe1f618a802ee4edb7c346b2785d"},{"id":"q-cc304cc10bc6","stem":"What is the acronym for the diffuse lymphatic tissue in the respiratory tract?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"BALT"},{"label":"B","text":"DALT"},{"label":"C","text":"FALT"},{"label":"D","text":"GALT"},{"label":"E","text":"HALT Diffuse lymphatic tissue is non-encapsulated lymphatic tissue. It is found in the gastrointestinal tract, the genito-urinary tract, and the respiratory tract. In the gastrointestinal tract it is referred to as GALT (gut associated lymphatic tissue). In the respiratory tract it is referred to as BALT (bronchi associated lymphatic tissue)."}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":true,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["repeated_question_stem","topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[],"duplicateStemGroup":"a8a93676df63dc210434434baf6ebb413f23ad37"},{"id":"q-2b4e3d571dd2","stem":"What are the localized concentrations of lymphocytes that are seen in the respiratory tract, genitourinary tract, and gastrointestinal tract?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Lymph follicles"},{"label":"B","text":"White pulp"},{"label":"C","text":"Peyer's patches"},{"label":"D","text":"Lymph node"},{"label":"E","text":"Diffuse lymphatic tissue The localized concentrations of lymphocytes that are seen in the respiratory tract, genitourinary tract, and gastrointestinal tract are lymph follicles. They are also called lymphatic nodules. The lymphatic tissue in the spleen is called white pulp. The large aggregates of lymphatic tissue in the ileum are called Peyer's patches. A lymph node is an encapsulated lymphatic organ. The random distribution of lymphocytes seen in the lamina propria of the respiratory tract, genitourinary tract, and gastrointestinal tract is called diffuse lymphatic tissue. Difuse lymphatic tissue in not encapsulated."}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":true,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["long_option_manual_review","repeated_question_stem","topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[],"duplicateStemGroup":"c36f54e07db1614e7f81105739e1ceb7f8117f94"},{"id":"q-270982d28fed","stem":"What is the lymphatic tissue in the spleen called?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Abdomen and GI Anatomy · Suggested","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":"Abdomen and GI Anatomy","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Lymph follicles"},{"label":"B","text":"White pulp"},{"label":"C","text":"Peyer's patches"},{"label":"D","text":"Lymph node"},{"label":"E","text":"Diffuse lymphatic tissue The localized concentrations of lymphocytes that are seen in the respiratory tract, genitourinary tract, and gastrointestinal tract are lymph follicles. They are also called lymphatic nodules. The lymphatic tissue in the spleen is called white pulp. The large aggregates of lymphatic tissue in the ileum are called Peyer's patches. A lymph node is an encapsulated lymphatic organ. The random distribution of lymphocytes seen in the lamina propria of the respiratory tract, genitourinary tract, and gastrointestinal tract is called diffuse lymphatic tissue. Difuse lymphatic tissue in not encapsulated."}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":true,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Abdomen and GI Anatomy · Suggested","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["long_option_manual_review","repeated_question_stem","topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[],"duplicateStemGroup":"7f04daa4053989b2e89c29419b35159ff618e194"},{"id":"q-72002452d181","stem":"When looking at the spleen, what are the invaginations of the capsule into the splenic parenchyma called?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Abdomen and GI Anatomy · Suggested","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":"Abdomen and GI Anatomy","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Malpighian corpuscle"},{"label":"B","text":"Trabeculae"},{"label":"C","text":"White pulp"},{"label":"D","text":"Red pulp"},{"label":"E","text":"Cords of Billroth The spleen has a connective tissue capsule. The invaginations of the capsule into the splenic parenchyma are trabeculae. The parenchyma of the spleen can be divided into the white pulp and the red pulp. The white pulp of the spleen is the lymphatic portion of the spleen. Within the white pulp, splenic nodules are found. Splenic nodules are also called Malpighian corpuscles. The red pulp is made up of the splenic sinuses and splenic cords. The splenic cords are also called the cords of Billroth."}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":true,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Abdomen and GI Anatomy · Suggested","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["long_option_manual_review","repeated_question_stem","topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[],"duplicateStemGroup":"2fc32229003643015288cd80a5d826c5ce1f3552"},{"id":"q-a9f25b7d1c23","stem":"When looking at a lymph node, which term does not refer to the same region as all the others listed?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Deep cortex"},{"label":"B","text":"Tertiary cortex"},{"label":"C","text":"Outer cortex"},{"label":"D","text":"Juxtamedullary cortex"},{"label":"E","text":"Paracortical zone terms for the same region in a lymph node. The deep cortex is the inner region of the cortex, next to the medulla."}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":true,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["repeated_question_stem","topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[],"duplicateStemGroup":"d1d7b133c647a11a226466fdef9a1e2ec495bc6b"},{"id":"q-67fb0aabcc0e","stem":"What are the spherical structures seen in the medulla of the thymus called?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Psammoma bodies"},{"label":"B","text":"Corpora arenacea"},{"label":"C","text":"Hassall's corpuscles"},{"label":"D","text":"Prostatic concretions"},{"label":"E","text":"Pacinian corpuscles Psammoma bodies are collections of calcium. It is derived from the Greek word \"psammos\", which means sand. Corpora arenacea refers to the calcifications seen in the pineal gland. Corpora arenacea is nicknamed \"brain sand\". Hassall's corpuscles are the ring like structures found in the thymus. The spherical structures seen in some prostatic alveoli are called prostatic concretions. Pacinian corpuscles are pressure receptors in the skin."}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":true,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["long_option_manual_review","repeated_question_stem","topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[],"duplicateStemGroup":"77200f966f2f78d0b44634dae6092cf8966330fe"},{"id":"q-3f7547e9ee33","stem":"Where are Peyer's patches located?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Esophagus"},{"label":"B","text":"Stomach"},{"label":"C","text":"Small intestine"},{"label":"D","text":"Large intestine"},{"label":"E","text":"Rectum Peyer's patches are large nodules of lymphatic tissue. They are seen in the small intestine."}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":true,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["repeated_question_stem","topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[],"duplicateStemGroup":"32cb3f3ba5ab3d43b471a36e0d2e70f63e7bf15a"},{"id":"q-101030a4885d","stem":"What are the large aggregates of lymphatic tissue in the ileum?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Abdomen and GI Anatomy · Suggested","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":"Abdomen and GI Anatomy","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Lymph follicles"},{"label":"B","text":"White pulp"},{"label":"C","text":"Peyer's patches"},{"label":"D","text":"Lymph node"},{"label":"E","text":"Diffuse lymphatic tissue genitourinary tract, and gastrointestinal tract are lymph follicles. They are also called lymphatic nodules. The lymphatic tissue in the spleen is called white pulp. The large aggregates of lymphatic tissue in the ileum are called Peyer's patches. A lymph node is an encapsulated lymphatic organ. The random distribution of lymphocytes seen in the lamina propria of the respiratory tract, genitourinary tract, and gastrointestinal tract is called diffuse lymphatic tissue. Difuse lymphatic tissue in not encapsulated."}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":true,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Abdomen and GI Anatomy · Suggested","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["long_option_manual_review","repeated_question_stem","topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[],"duplicateStemGroup":"be6a1996c8c4f018c6b8662900fc0fdd75c0542f"},{"id":"q-9617bbd793d8","stem":"Where are the splenic sinuses?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Malpighian corpuscle"},{"label":"B","text":"Trabeculae"},{"label":"C","text":"White pulp"},{"label":"D","text":"Red pulp"},{"label":"E","text":"Cords of Billroth The spleen has a connective tissue capsule. The invaginations of the capsule into the splenic parenchyma are trabeculae. The parenchyma of the spleen can be divided into the white pulp and the red pulp. The white pulp of the spleen is the lymphatic portion of the spleen. Within the white pulp, splenic nodules are found. Splenic nodules are also called Malpighian corpuscles. The red pulp is made up of the splenic sinuses and splenic cords. The splenic cords are also called the cords of Billroth."}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":true,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["long_option_manual_review","repeated_question_stem","topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[],"duplicateStemGroup":"56da7a25e7580313728bd95b955872728e768b88"},{"id":"q-8d801433f17e","stem":"Which of the following is an encapsulated lymphatic organ?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Lymph follicles"},{"label":"B","text":"White pulp"},{"label":"C","text":"Peyer's patches"},{"label":"D","text":"Lymph node"},{"label":"E","text":"Diffuse lymphatic tissue The localized concentrations of lymphocytes that are seen in the respiratory tract, genitourinary tract, and gastrointestinal tract are lymph follicles. They are also called lymphatic nodules. The lymphatic tissue in the spleen is called white pulp. The large aggregates of lymphatic tissue in the ileum are called Peyer's patches. A lymph node is an encapsulated lymphatic organ. The random distribution of lymphocytes seen in the lamina propria of the respiratory tract, genitourinary tract, and gastrointestinal tract is called diffuse lymphatic tissue. Difuse lymphatic tissue in not encapsulated."}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":true,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["long_option_manual_review","repeated_question_stem","topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[],"duplicateStemGroup":"d247ab4995a46b7891fe334b86f8ea727f18b6dc"},{"id":"q-9a5067f2fd2a","stem":"Syntoses are? Fibrous joints Synovial joints Syndesmoses Ossified sutures None of the above","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Unclassified Health Sciences","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["subject_unclassified","topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Unclassified Health Sciences","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"review_required","auditFlags":["subject_needs_classification","subject_unclassified","topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-a8e2aeaf2a1f","stem":"A lateral blow on extended knee could cause damage to what structures? Tibial collateral ligament Medial meniscus ACL None of above All of the above","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Unclassified Health Sciences","topic":"U