fi bres"},{"label":"B","text":"Serous and mucus membranes and lamina propria"},{"label":"C","text":"Protein fi bres and ground substance"},{"label":"D","text":"Interstitial fl uid"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["repeated_question_stem","topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":["option_whitespace_normalization","source answer/boilerplate removed from option D"],"duplicateStemGroup":"f0c5765aeac651da0e1b2ffcbdf19ce6898c7c3d"},{"id":"q-a9d10b842ec9","stem":"Which statement about the plasma membrane is INCORRECT ?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"It is selectively permeable."},{"label":"B","text":"It is composed of two layers of glycoprotein molecules."},{"label":"C","text":"It contains receptors for specifi c signalling molecules. 2 Cells and Tissues 11"},{"label":"D","text":"The plasma membranes of adjacent cells are held together by desmosomes."}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["repeated_question_stem","topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":["option_whitespace_normalization","source answer/boilerplate removed from option D"],"duplicateStemGroup":"ad489fd923ef7e5376fdc9a4d57f1856d80b5143"},{"id":"q-08f7006f9f7a","stem":"What tissue has cells that are closely packed and that have one surface attached to a basement membrane and the other free to a space?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"epithelial tissue"},{"label":"B","text":"muscle tissue"},{"label":"C","text":"connective tissue"},{"label":"D","text":"nervous tissue"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"independent_no_source_key_bulk_v4","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"provisional_answer","auditFlags":["repeated_question_stem","topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":["option_whitespace_normalization","source answer/boilerplate removed from option D"],"duplicateStemGroup":"f512665032891533a9dca4d41afba06c13bc6786"},{"id":"q-f9024c050e6b","stem":"What is the name of the mechanism that ensures that there is a higher concen- tration of sodium ions in the extracellular fl uid than in the intracellular fl uid?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Facilitated diffusion"},{"label":"B","text":"The sodium-potassium pump"},{"label":"C","text":"Secondary active transport"},{"label":"D","text":"Osmosis"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["repeated_question_stem","topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":["source answer/boilerplate removed from option D","whitespace/page_marker_normalization"],"duplicateStemGroup":"42a6fd1120055289825e794930060354b3ef4ab1"},{"id":"q-067dd3d4a8e4","stem":"What is the function of phospholipids in the plasma membrane?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"to maintain the intracellular fl uid at a similar composition to that of the interstitial fl uid."},{"label":"B","text":"to form channels to selectively allow passage of small molecules."},{"label":"C","text":"to act as receptors for signalling chemicals."},{"label":"D","text":"to present a barrier to the passage of water-soluble molecules."}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["repeated_question_stem","topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":["option_whitespace_normalization","source answer/boilerplate removed from option D"],"duplicateStemGroup":"a560226bf56f0ae704f1160bec549882a74b00d4"},{"id":"q-fe35966eeaf3","stem":"Which of the following is NOT part of the plasma membrane of a cell?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"integral proteins"},{"label":"B","text":"glycoproteins"},{"label":"C","text":"plasma proteins"},{"label":"D","text":"peripheral proteins"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["repeated_question_stem","topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":["source answer/boilerplate removed from option D"],"duplicateStemGroup":"1b5cc8bfdf0497cd20058f02ce833e6a51f9307b"},{"id":"q-86b5130d7105","stem":"What is the purpose of mitochondria?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"to store the nucleolus and chromatin"},{"label":"B","text":"to produce adenosine triphosphate 2 Cells and Tissues 15"},{"label":"C","text":"to support and shape the cell."},{"label":"D","text":"they produce enzymes to break down molecules"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["repeated_question_stem","topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":["source answer/boilerplate removed from option D"],"duplicateStemGroup":"480eae0719b068188d98041d00378bfa7f580b94"},{"id":"q-0302d672239b","stem":"What do fi broblasts, chondroblasts, osteoblasts and haemocytoblasts have in common?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"they are all types of white blood cell."},{"label":"B","text":"they are all macrophages."},{"label":"C","text":"they are all immature cells."},{"label":"D","text":"they are all types of epithelial cell."}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["repeated_question_stem","topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":["option_whitespace_normalization","source answer/boilerplate removed from option D","whitespace/page_marker_normalization"],"duplicateStemGroup":"9b9244c20455e6a4512710ff82bcde9c26565862"},{"id":"q-e853649bae19","stem":"Which is NOT true of connective tissue (CT)?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"the cells are closely packed"},{"label":"B","text":"the tissue contains protein fi bres and ground substance."},{"label":"C","text":"types include loose CT, dense CT and liquid CT."},{"label":"D","text":"CT contains white blood cells."}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["repeated_question_stem","topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":["option_whitespace_normalization","source answer/boilerplate removed from option D"],"duplicateStemGroup":"271048d9d4dbf709df13c15524550abe8f40f52a"},{"id":"q-e761e802bb5a","stem":"Active transport across the plasma membrane may be described by which statement?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Thoracic Wall and Ribs","topicOriginal":"Thoracic Wall and Ribs","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"active transport requires energy from ATP."},{"label":"B","text":"active transport is also known as endocytosis."},{"label":"C","text":"active transport moves molecules along their concentration gradient."},{"label":"D","text":"active transport is the movement of lipid-soluble molecules through the plasma membrane."}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Thoracic Wall and Ribs","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["answe