ut not part of the SI system."},{"label":"B","text":"It is part of the SI system but not part of the Australian Metric System."},{"label":"C","text":"It belongs to both the SI system and the Australian Metric System."},{"label":"D","text":"It does not belong to either the SI system or the Australian Metric System."}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["repeated_question_stem","topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":["source answer/boilerplate removed from option D"],"duplicateStemGroup":"de3727eb34d16a11e100165db9e484c0b0b5691b"},{"id":"q-9b28c63e8fe1","stem":"The chemical formula C6H12O6 contains much information. However, what information is NOT provided by the formula?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"the number of atoms in a molecule"},{"label":"B","text":"the name of the substance 44"},{"label":"C","text":"the elements that make up the substance"},{"label":"D","text":"whether the substance is covalent or ionic"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["repeated_question_stem","topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":["source answer/boilerplate removed from option D"],"duplicateStemGroup":"e3d7463dad86dcfd4aa53a8fbc6e422a6914c5d1"},{"id":"q-034d18133810","stem":"One of the following is INCORRECT. Which one?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"metal atoms will form compounds with non-metal atoms."},{"label":"B","text":"metal atoms will form compounds with metal atoms."},{"label":"C","text":"non-metal atoms will form compounds with non-metal atoms."},{"label":"D","text":"metal atoms will not form compounds with metal atoms."}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["repeated_question_stem","topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":["option_whitespace_normalization","source answer/boilerplate removed from option D"],"duplicateStemGroup":"7daef4b2129576a98dba3b366db1332357c9b1b8"},{"id":"q-202361b4c657","stem":"What happens when a sodium atom reacts to form a compound? The atom will","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"gain one electron"},{"label":"B","text":"lose one electron"},{"label":"C","text":"gain two electrons"},{"label":"D","text":"lose two electrons"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["repeated_question_stem","topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":["option_whitespace_normalization","source answer/boilerplate removed from option D"],"duplicateStemGroup":"4743b114c60d388bebb9911f3e0f365ab287d61b"},{"id":"q-9fcafd630d6b","stem":"“Covalent” is the term applied to which of the following bonds?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Those between an ion and all the surrounding oppositely charged ions."},{"label":"B","text":"The bond between an electrolyte and the surrounding water molecules in a solution."},{"label":"C","text":"Those between non-metal atoms."},{"label":"D","text":"Those between atoms on the left hand side of the periodic table."}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"independent_no_source_key_bulk_v4","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"provisional_answer","auditFlags":["repeated_question_stem","topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":["option_whitespace_normalization","source answer/boilerplate removed from option D"],"duplicateStemGroup":"3e4336773dc6bac6531ce116d8fed39678d9667d"},{"id":"q-5aceab75ef66","stem":"Two atoms have the same mass number but different atomic numbers. Which of the following statements concerning these atoms is TRUE?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Each has the same number of neutrons in its nucleus."},{"label":"B","text":"They are isotopes."},{"label":"C","text":"They are atoms of different elements."},{"label":"D","text":"Each has the same number of protons in its nucleus."}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["repeated_question_stem","topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":["option_whitespace_normalization","source answer/boilerplate removed from option D"],"duplicateStemGroup":"c796a6121e5a17d94b049026cb13d6d0df159583"},{"id":"q-b63cb63240ae","stem":"What does the term “electrolyte” refer to?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"the minor component of a solution."},{"label":"B","text":"a substance that will conduct electricity when dissolved in water. 4 Chemistry for Physiology 55"},{"label":"C","text":"the smallest particle of an element."},{"label":"D","text":"negatively charged sub-atomic particles."}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["repeated_question_stem","topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":["option_whitespace_normalization","source answer/boilerplate removed from option D"],"duplicateStemGroup":"728b4ebae574a463aa2bd501d93d317d77a7cac0"},{"id":"q-248d4f9e1342","stem":"Which strategy would be most effective in dealing with a severe case of dehydration?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Oral administration of a hypertonic solution."},{"label":"B","text":"Intravenous administration of distilled water."},{"label":"C","text":"Intravenous administration of isotonic glucose."},{"label":"D","text":"Intravenous administration of hypotonic sodium chloride."}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["repeated_question_stem","topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":["option_whitespace_normalization","source answer/boilerplate removed from option D"],"duplicateStemGroup":"be24c4b4912935fd2d9049e930b1c458fc4469f7"},{"id":"q-49fa79241dcc","stem":"To what condition does the term “hypokalemia” refer?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"too little phosphorus in the blood"},{"label":"B","text":"too much sodium in the blood"},{"label":"C","text":"too little potassium in the blood"},{"label":"D","text":"too little sodium in the blood"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["repeated_question_stem","topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":["option_whitespace_normalization","source answer/boilerplate removed from option D"],"duplicateStemGroup":"c69e294a91a933b2eebda9f7bad3c164b594a3cb"},{"id":"q-90bfd093cdd3","stem":"What may hyponatremia be described as?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Thoracic Wall and Ribs","topicOriginal":"Thoracic Wall and Ribs","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"insufficient potassium in the blood"},{"label":"B","text":"insufficient iron in the blood"},{"label":"C","text":"insufficient sodium in the blood"},{"label":"D","text":"excess sodium in the blood"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Thoracic Wall and Ribs","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["repeated_question_stem"],"cleaningOperations":["option_whitespace_normalization","source answer/boilerplate removed from option D"],"duplicateStemGroup":"11a9a5a30fc943232e1b27ae8ae461f2053b2f52"},{"id":"q-bbe65ae90605","stem":"3 Diffusion and Osmosis 62 Another way to describe the concentration of a solution is to state its “osmotic pressure” (in pressure units). The osmotic pressure of blood is about 7.3 atmo- spheres (740 kPa). Osmotic pressure is described as the tendency of water to move into a solution via osmosis. The higher the solution’s concentration, the higher is its osmotic pressure. Osmotic pressure may be measured by determining the amount of pressure that must be applied to a solution to prevent water from entering the solu- tion by osmosis.","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Thoracic Wall and Ribs","topicOriginal":"Thoracic Wall and Ribs","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Thoracic Wall and Ribs","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-87cf82d0d08f","stem":"What is the movement of water molecules across a plasma membrane from the side where the solution concentration is more dilute to the side where the solu- tion is more concentrated called?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"osmosis"},{"label":"B","text":"reverse osmosis"},{"label":"C","text":"diffusion"},{"label":"D","text":"hydration"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["repeated_question_stem","topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":["option_whitespace_normalization","source answer/boilerplate removed from option D"],"duplicateStemGroup":"eb24b6f83e5eec9c23391d13ad4a862d06c14444"},{"id":"q-b9a214e968de","stem":"What does “osmosis” refer to?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"the constant random motion of ions and molecules."},{"label":"B","text":"the movement of ions and molecules from regions of high concentration to regions of low concentration."},{"label":"C","text":"the movement of water molecules through a semi-permeable membrane. 4 Chemistry for Physiology 65"},{"label":"D","text":"the movement of water molecules through a semi-permeable membrane from the side with higher water concentration to the side with lower water concentration."}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["repeated_question_stem","topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":["option_whitespace_normalization","source answer/boilerplate removed from option D"],"duplicateStemGroup":"72a0485fe26ef08e337b9378d47ab8ce125d3c60"},{"id":"q-f7f54c72c795","stem":"Consider a patient undergoing kidney dialysis, whose blood has bicarbonate at a concentration of 14 mmol/L and urea at 23 mmol/L. The dialysing liquid has bicarbonate at 32 mmol/L and urea at 0 mmol/L. In which direction will these substances flow? 4 Chemistry for Physiology 67","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"bicarbonate will flow from patient’s blood to dialysing liquid, urea will flow from patient’s blood into dialysing liquid."},{"label":"B","text":"bicarbonate will flow from dialysing liquid to patient’s blood, urea will flow from dialysing liquid into patient’s blood."},{"label":"C","text":"bicarbonate will flow from dialysing liquid to patient’s blood, urea will flow from patient’s blood into dialysing liquid."},{"label":"D","text":"bicarbonate will flow from patient’s blood to dialysing liquid, urea will flow from dialysing liquid to patient’s blood."}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["repeated_question_stem","topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":["source answer/boilerplate removed from option D"],"duplicateStemGroup":"529a30e03d4909986e3c955463ce1b2e2986b0f8"},{"id":"q-e7b0071e7945","stem":"The Na+/K+ ATPase pump in the plasma membrane moves Na out of the cell and K into the cell against their concentration gradient. Then Na reenters the cell and K leaks out of the cell, along their concentration gradients through their membrane channels. What is the movement of Na and K along their concentra- tion gradients called? 4 Chemistry for Physiology 69","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Active transport"},{"label":"B","text":"Diffusion"},{"label":"C","text":"Facilitated diffusion"},{"label":"D","text":"Osmosis"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["repeated_question_stem","topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":["source answer/boilerplate removed from option D"],"duplicateStemGroup":"08ff83f2d26c528f8397cfc31526efdc5ee37bf7"},{"id":"q-332773450fef","stem":"What can be correctly said of an isotonic intravenous solution? An isotonic solution","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"causes water to move out of red blood cells."},{"label":"B","text":"causes no net movement of water into or out of red blood cells."},{"label":"C","text":"has the same solutes in the same solution concentration as blood plasma 4 Chemistry for Physiology 71"},{"label":"D","text":"causes water to move into red blood cells."}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["repeated_question_stem","topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":["option_whitespace_normalization","source answer/boilerplate removed from option D"],"duplicateStemGroup":"2c558d5db9ded86b4e9a2870d0549554ce2d980f"},{"id":"q-86153766a457","stem":"Given that one mole of Na+Cl− has a mass of 58.5 g, what would be the concen- tration of particles when 0.9 g of Na+Cl− is dissolved in 100 ml of water?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"117 mmol/L"},{"label":"B","text":"150 mmol/L"},{"label":"C","text":"150 mosmol/L"},{"label":"D","text":"300 mosmol/L Ans:=D: the number of moles in 0.9 g = 0.9 g/58.5 g/mol = 0.0154 mole of Na+Cl−. However NaCl separates into ions when dissolved, so that there will be"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["repeated_question_stem","topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":["option_whitespace_normalization"],"duplicateStemGroup":"59518de878b375e1b7b2ea1419a7d71fc31681a6"},{"id":"q-8f12f7d2da95","stem":"To what does the term “mole” refer?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"the smallest particle of a molecular compound."},{"label":"B","text":"the amount of solute that must be dissolved in water to make an isotonic solution."},{"label":"C","text":"an amount of substance that contains 6.02 × 1023 particles."},{"label":"D","text":"a group of 2 or more atoms bonded together."}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["repeated_question_stem","topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":["option_whitespace_normalization","source answer/boilerplate removed from option D"],"duplicateStemGroup":"8764069dd8c8ac1ddb4753f486e5afa2ad50c741"},{"id":"q-4f0d102323c4","stem":"Human blood has an osmolarity that lies within the range 280– 300 mosmol/L. Which of the following statements is correct?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"an isotonic solution has osmolarity that is either less than 280 or greater than 300 mosmol/L"},{"label":"B","text":"a hypotonic solution has osmolarity between 280 and 300 mosmol/L 4 Chemistry for Physiology 77"},{"label":"C","text":"a hypertonic solution has osmolarity between 280 and 300 mosmol/L"},{"label":"D","text":"an isotonic solution has osmolarity between 280 and 300 mosmol/L"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["repeated_question_stem","topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":["option_whitespace_normalization","source answer/boilerplate removed from option D"],"duplicateStemGroup":"718d25ca99d028018ddc30f9be04fc82863393de"},{"id":"q-ba0adc520b00","stem":"Which of the following statements best describes what all isotonic solutions have in common? They","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Thoracic Wall and Ribs","topicOriginal":"Thoracic Wall and Ribs","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"contain the same concentration of glucose molecules"},{"label":"B","text":"contain the same total concentration of particles"},{"label":"C","text":"contain the same concentration of sodium chloride"},{"label":"D","text":"have the same specific gravity"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Thoracic Wall and Ribs","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["repeated_question_stem"],"cleaningOperations":["option_whitespace_normalization","source answer/boilerplate removed from option D"],"duplicateStemGroup":"425e24882dee90b62e4827229cc905052118427c"},{"id":"q-c3c9a74af6e3","stem":"A buffer solution consisting of citric acid and citrate in a ratio of 5:1 maintains the pH at 7.4. What would be the ratio of the two components if the pH were to become 6.4","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"6:1"},{"label":"B","text":"10:1"},{"label":"C","text":"50:1"},{"label":"D","text":"1:10"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"exact_duplicate_projected_provisional_oct20","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"provisional_answer","auditFlags":["repeated_question_stem","topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":["option_whitespace_normalization","source answer/boilerplate removed from option D"],"duplicateStemGroup":"00bf77cccdeec2987aaf288c7b982063ce8164bf"},{"id":"q-92271461eb10","stem":"If a sample of blood has a pH of 7.25, which of the following is true?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"it is in the healthy range"},{"label":"B","text":"it is neutral 4 Chemistry for Physiology 87"},{"label":"C","text":"it is acidic"},{"label":"D","text":"it is basic"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"independent_no_source_key_bulk_v4","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"provisional_answer","auditFlags":["repeated_question_stem","topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":["option_whitespace_normalization","source answer/boilerplate removed from option D"],"duplicateStemGroup":"450d2cc87bcc6653dcaf2ed46b4380ab15a81539"},{"id":"q-f0e156281a6f","stem":"What effect would an acid have when added to the following solutions?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Tap water: – it would react to produce hydronium ions."},{"label":"B","text":"Tap water: – it would react to produce hydroxide ions."},{"label":"C","text":"Buffered water: – it would lower the pH of the solution. 4 Chemistry for Physiology 91"},{"label":"D","text":"Un-buffered water: it would raise the pH of the solution."}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["repeated_question_stem","topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":["option_whitespace_normalization","source answer/boilerplate removed from option D"],"duplicateStemGroup":"41c9bfb7b5366f2df504ce653c6d559cafd26fad"},{"id":"q-293e7d886b0b","stem":"A buffer solution having a pH of 8.5 has a small amount of strong acid added to it. What is its pH value likely to be closest to? 4 Chemistry for Physiology 93","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"5.0"},{"label":"B","text":"8.4"},{"label":"C","text":"8.6"},{"label":"D","text":"12.4"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["repeated_question_stem","topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":["source answer/boilerplate removed from option D"],"duplicateStemGroup":"536a37056eb1aef8287425f80fa3ab1e111219c2"},{"id":"q-e3d1cff968f1","stem":"6 Organic Chemistry and Macromolecules 94 give it its characteristic shape – this is the tertiary structure. If several polypeptide chains (each with their own primary, secondary and tertiary structure) interact to form the protein – as is the case for haemoglobin – this is called their quaternary structure. Carbohydrates (such as starch, glycogen, cellulose) are composed of monosaccharides (such as glucose, fructose, galactose, ribose). Two monosaccha- rides can join to form a 12 carbon atom disaccharide such as sucrose, lactose or maltose.","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Thoracic Wall and Ribs","topicOriginal":"Thoracic Wall and Ribs","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Thoracic Wall and Ribs","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-e2c545f22421","stem":"Which of the following is the correct combination for the nucleic acid DNA?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Phosphate, Ribose, Uracil"},{"label":"B","text":"Phosphate, Deoxyribose, Uracil"},{"label":"C","text":"Phosphate, Ribose, Adenine"},{"label":"D","text":"Phosphate, Deoxyribose, Adenine"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["repeated_question_stem","topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":["option_whitespace_normalization","source answer/boilerplate removed from option D"],"duplicateStemGroup":"ac470778543b681d809afc92bdc1b6b201f62707"},{"id":"q-88e57e4b4e47","stem":"Which of the following chemical formulae represents a typical carbohydrate?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ca10(PO4)6(OH)2"},{"label":"B","text":"N2C5H12O4"},{"label":"C","text":"C120H240O120 4 Chemistry for Physiology 97"},{"label":"D","text":"C57H110O6"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["repeated_question_stem","topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":["option_whitespace_normalization","source answer/boilerplate removed from option D"],"duplicateStemGroup":"ca02c96df0636cf0fc50be4089f3f3b72c3ff0f9"},{"id":"q-1a69c663a326","stem":"What is a “saturated fat”?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"one that contains cholesterol"},{"label":"B","text":"a triglyceride that has three fatty acids"},{"label":"C","text":"one where the carbon atoms that are connected by single bonds 4 Chemistry for Physiology 99"},{"label":"D","text":"one that must be included in our diet"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"independent_no_source_key_bulk_v4","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"provisional_answer","auditFlags":["repeated_question_stem","topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":["option_whitespace_normalization","source answer/boilerplate removed from option D"],"duplicateStemGroup":"c6ba30cb2c3dbbddd3d3e8594e7e863421b64140"},{"id":"q-32cb80122508","stem":"Only one of the following patterns of bond is ever displayed by carbon atoms in organic compounds. (each line represents a bond) Which is it? A. | ─ C ≡ B. ǁ ─ C ─ | C. | ─ C ═ D. ─ C ≡ |","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":["source answer/boilerplate removed from stem"]},{"id":"q-af20cafb4ca4","stem":"Certain components of food are often referred to as being saturated. This term refers to the bonding of carbon atoms present in molecules of the food. Which of the following bonding arrangements is a saturated environment? B. C. D.","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-d98f0e6c695f","stem":"Which statement below is correct?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"dendrites are produced by dendrocytes"},{"label":"B","text":"glycolipids are produced by lipocytes"},{"label":"C","text":"keratin is produced by keratinocytes"},{"label":"D","text":"melanin is produced by merkel cells"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["repeated_question_stem","topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":["source answer/boilerplate removed from option D"],"duplicateStemGroup":"7418c3ce05d8540725d6dacd95c1e96e352af87b"},{"id":"q-469c6c932ff3","stem":"Which is a notable feature of the stratum corneum layer of the integument? It","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"is where melanocytes and keratinocytes are rapidly dividing."},{"label":"B","text":"is richly supplied with blood capillaries."},{"label":"C","text":"consists of keratin fi lled cells with glycolipid in between cells."},{"label":"D","text":"has protruding epidermal ridges that push the overlying epidermis into “fi ngerprints”."}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["repeated_question_stem","topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":["option_whitespace_normalization","source answer/boilerplate removed from option D"],"duplicateStemGroup":"df0dacedb267cc0efe8e2fa69747db47a8e793ab"},{"id":"q-b9cdbfd4ee15","stem":"When 